// Interview problems: Polymers (27-777): Gedde exercises, McCrum problems and course-style problems. Fields: id, title, q (statement), figs, figDesc, sol (examiner-only reference), answers.
(window.EXAMPLES_BY=window.EXAMPLES_BY||{}).poly=[{"id":"1.1","title":"Average molar masses of a blended polystyrene","q":"A polystyrene sample is made by blending eight fractions, each with a very narrow molar mass distribution. Mass (g) and molar mass (g/mol): 0.10 g of 15 000; 0.19 g of 27 000; 0.25 g of 39 000; 0.17 g of 56 000; 0.12 g of 78 000; 0.08 g of 104 000; 0.06 g of 120 000; 0.04 g of 153 000. Calculate the number-average molar mass, the weight-average molar mass and the dispersity of the blend.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use Mn = Σw_i / Σ(w_i/M_i) and Mw = Σ w_i M_i / Σ w_i, with w_i the mass of each fraction (total W = 1.01 g).\n2. Σ(w_i/M_i) = 6.667e-6 + 7.037e-6 + 6.410e-6 + 3.036e-6 + 1.538e-6 + 0.769e-6 + 0.500e-6 + 0.261e-6 = 2.622e-5 mol.\n3. Mn = 1.01 / 2.622e-5 = 3.85e4 g/mol.\n4. Σ w_i M_i = 1500 + 5130 + 9750 + 9520 + 9360 + 8320 + 7200 + 6120 = 56 900 (g·g/mol), so Mw = 56 900/1.01 = 5.63e4 g/mol.\n5. Dispersity D = Mw/Mn = 5.63e4/3.85e4 = 1.46.\nThe book answer key (Mn = 38 687, Mw = 56 630) differs by under 0.5 % from this arithmetic.","answers":["Mn ≈ 3.85×10⁴ g/mol","Mw ≈ 5.63×10⁴ g/mol","Dispersity ≈ 1.46"],"src":"McCrum Problem 1.15"},{"id":"1.2","title":"Effect of residual monomer on molar mass averages","q":"To the polystyrene blend of the previous problem (total mass 1.01 g, Mn = 3.85×10⁴ g/mol, Mw = 5.63×10⁴ g/mol), styrene monomer (molar mass 104 g/mol) is added so that it makes up 0.5 wt% of the final mixture. Calculate the new Mn and Mw and say which average is more affected and why.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: treat monomer as one more fraction. Total mass T = 1.01/0.995 = 1.01508 g, so monomer mass = 0.00508 g.\n2. Number average: Mn = T / Σ(w_i/M_i). The monomer adds 0.00508/104 = 4.88e-5 mol to the previous 2.622e-5 mol, giving 7.50e-5 mol.\n3. Mn = 1.01508 / 7.50e-5 = 1.35e4 g/mol.\n4. Weight average: Σ w_i M_i = 56 900 + 0.00508 × 104 = 56 900.5; Mw = 56 900.5 / 1.01508 = 5.61e4 g/mol.\n5. Mn drops by about 65 % because the monomer is a very large number of molecules; Mw barely changes (-0.5 %) because it weights by mass, and the monomer carries little mass.\nBook values: Mn = 13 595, Mw = 56 349.","answers":["Mn ≈ 1.35×10⁴ g/mol (drops by about 65 %)","Mw ≈ 5.6×10⁴ g/mol (nearly unchanged)","Mn is much more sensitive to low-molar-mass species, Mw to heavy chains"],"src":"McCrum Problem 1.16"},{"id":"1.3","title":"Chain length limited by an end-capping impurity","q":"Nylon 6,6 is made by reacting 100 mol hexamethylene diamine (116 g/mol) with 99 mol adipic acid (146 g/mol) in the presence of 2 mol acetic acid (60 g/mol), which has only one reactive group. Assume every amine and acid group reacts and each condensation step releases one water molecule (18 g/mol). Calculate the resulting number-average molar mass.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: count functional groups and condensation events, then divide the mass of the reaction mixture minus the water lost by the number of chains.\n2. Amine groups: 2 × 100 = 200. Acid groups: 2 × 99 + 2 = 200. All 200 pairs react, so 200 water molecules are released.\n3. Molecules initially: 100 + 99 + 2 = 201; each condensation removes one molecule, so 201 - 200 = 1 chain remains, with an acetyl cap on each end because the acid groups of acetic acid terminate the chain.\n4. Mass of ingredients: 100×116 + 99×146 + 2×60 = 11 600 + 14 454 + 120 = 26 174 g.\n5. Mass of water lost: 200 × 18 = 3600 g.\n6. Mn = (26 174 - 3600)/1 mol = 22 574 g/mol.\nThe monofunctional impurity and the 100:99 imbalance cap the chain length (Carothers limit).","answers":["Mn ≈ 2.26×10⁴ g/mol (22 574 g/mol)","The chain is end-capped by the monofunctional acid, so growth stops"],"src":"McCrum Problem 1.12"},{"id":"1.4","title":"Mole fractions of units in industrial copolymers","q":"Calculate the mole percent of the named monomer units in two copolymers specified by weight composition: (a) styrene-butadiene rubber with 25 wt% styrene (styrene 104 g/mol, butadiene 54 g/mol), and (b) nitrile rubber, a butadiene-acrylonitrile copolymer with 40 wt% acrylonitrile (53 g/mol).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: mol fraction of A = (w_A/M_A) / (w_A/M_A + w_B/M_B), using 100 g of copolymer.\n2. (a) styrene: 25/104 = 0.2404 mol; butadiene: 75/54 = 1.3889 mol. Total = 1.6293 mol, so styrene = 0.2404/1.6293 = 14.75 mol%.\n3. (b) acrylonitrile: 40/53 = 0.7547 mol; butadiene: 60/54 = 1.1111 mol. Total = 1.8658 mol, so acrylonitrile = 0.7547/1.8658 = 40.4 mol%.\n4. Note that the heavy styrene unit makes the mole fraction much smaller than the weight fraction, while the two units in (b) have similar mass, so the fractions nearly agree.","answers":["Styrene: 14.75 mol%","Acrylonitrile: 40.4 mol%"],"src":"McCrum Problem 1.9"},{"id":"1.5","title":"Styrene content and domains of a triblock rubber","q":"A thermoplastic rubber is a triblock copolymer with a central polybutadiene block of degree of polymerization 2000 and two polystyrene end blocks of degree of polymerization 150 each. In the solid, the polystyrene blocks form spherical domains of diameter 25 nm. Use molar masses 104 g/mol (styrene) and 54 g/mol (butadiene) and a polystyrene density of 1050 kg/m³. Calculate the weight fraction of styrene and estimate how many polystyrene end blocks sit in one sphere.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: weight fraction from block masses; domain mass from volume and density, divided by the mass of one block.\n2. Mass of the two PS blocks per chain: 2 × 150 × 104 = 31 200 g/mol. Butadiene block: 2000 × 54 = 108 000 g/mol. Total = 139 200 g/mol.\n3. Weight fraction of styrene = 31 200/139 200 = 0.224.\n4. Sphere volume: V = (4/3)π(12.5×10⁻⁹ m)³ = 8.18×10⁻²⁴ m³; mass = 8.18×10⁻²⁴ × 1050 = 8.59×10⁻²¹ kg.\n5. Mass of one PS end block: 150 × 104 g/mol = 15.6 kg/mol, divided by N_A = 6.02×10²³ gives 2.59×10⁻²⁶ kg.\n6. Number of end blocks per sphere = 8.59×10⁻²¹ / 2.59×10⁻²⁶ ≈ 332.\nEach sphere is a physical crosslink tying together hundreds of rubbery polybutadiene chains.","answers":["Styrene weight fraction ≈ 0.224","About 330 polystyrene blocks per sphere (332)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 1.14"},{"id":"1.6","title":"Which polymers can crystallize, and why","q":"A designer must decide which of the following polymers could be semicrystalline: (a) atactic polystyrene; (b) isotactic polypropylene; (c) a statistical (random) ethylene-propylene copolymer with about 50 mol% of each unit; (d) all-trans polyisoprene (gutta-percha) versus all-cis polyisoprene (natural rubber); (e) polystyrene made by free-radical polymerization. For each, state whether crystallization is expected and give the structural reason. Also state which molecular feature a 13C NMR spectrum of polypropylene reveals.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: crystallization needs a regular, repeating configuration so that chains can pack in a lattice.\n2. (a) No: atactic means random stereocenters and bulky phenyl groups, so the chain is irregular and freezes into a glass.\n3. (b) Yes: all stereocenters identical (meso dyads), chains form regular helices and pack into crystals; it is a semicrystalline engineering plastic.\n4. (c) No in practice: irregular sequence of units, the random copolymer is amorphous (and rubbery, EPR) at most compositions.\n5. (d) Gutta-percha (trans) is regular and crystallizes; natural rubber (cis) has kinked chains and remains amorphous and rubbery at room temperature.\n6. (e) Free-radical polymerization gives atactic PS (not stereocontrolled), so it does not crystallize; isotactic PS needs a Ziegler-Natta type catalyst.\n7. 13C NMR resolves dyads, triads, tetrads, pentads (mm, mr, rr, ...), i.e. the tacticity.","answers":["(a) no, (b) yes, (c) no, (d) trans yes, cis no, (e) atactic, no","Regular configuration is required for crystallization","13C NMR gives the tacticity (meso/racemic sequences)"],"src":"Gedde Exercises 1.4, 1.6 (course-style extension)"},{"id":"1.7","title":"Number-average molar mass from osmotic pressure","q":"The osmotic pressure of a dilute polymer solution at 27 °C (300 K) and concentration 4.0 g/L is measured as 333 Pa. Assume the concentration is low enough that the ideal limit Π/c = RT/Mn applies. Calculate Mn. Light scattering on the same sample gives Mw = 1.2×10⁵ g/mol. Find the dispersity and the standard deviation of the molar mass distribution, and explain why osmometry gives Mn and not Mw. Use R = 8.314 J/(mol K).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: colligative property depends on the number of solute molecules per volume; Mn = c R T / Π.\n2. c = 4.0 g/L = 4.0 kg/m³.\n3. Mn = (4.0 kg/m³)(8.314)(300)/333 Pa = 29.96 kg/mol ≈ 3.0×10⁴ g/mol.\n4. Dispersity D = Mw/Mn = 1.2×10⁵/3.0×10⁴ = 4.0.\n5. From D = 1 + σ²/Mn²: σ = Mn √(D - 1) = 3.0×10⁴ × √3 = 5.2×10⁴ g/mol, larger than Mn itself, so the distribution is very broad.\n6. Osmotic pressure counts molecules: with ΣN_i = A molecules and total mass B = ΣN_iM_i, the average is B/A = Mn; heavy and light chains contribute equally per molecule.","answers":["Mn ≈ 3.0×10⁴ g/mol","D ≈ 4.0","σ ≈ 5.2×10⁴ g/mol","Colligative properties count molecules, so they give Mn"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 1.8 (course-style numbers)"},{"id":"2.1","title":"Size of a very long polyethylene coil under theta conditions","q":"Calculate the root-mean-square end-to-end distance of a polyethylene chain of molar mass 10⁷ g/mol under theta conditions at 140 °C, taking the characteristic ratio C∞ = 6.7, the C-C bond length l = 0.154 nm and the CH2 repeat unit as 14 g/mol with one backbone bond per repeat unit. Compare it with the length of the fully extended planar zigzag chain (bond angle 109.5°).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use ⟨R²⟩0 = C∞ n l² for the ideal (unperturbed) coil, and the zigzag contour L = n l sin(τ/2) with τ the bond angle.\n2. Number of bonds: n = 10⁷/14 = 7.14×10⁵.\n3. ⟨R²⟩0 = 6.7 × 7.14×10⁵ × (0.154 nm)² = 6.7 × 7.14×10⁵ × 0.02372 = 1.135×10⁵ nm².\n4. R = √⟨R²⟩0 = 337 nm.\n5. Extended length: L = 7.14×10⁵ × 0.154 × sin(54.75°) = 7.14×10⁵ × 0.154 × 0.8165 = 8.98×10⁴ nm ≈ 90 μm.\n6. Ratio R/L = 337/89 800 = 0.0038, i.e. the coil is about 270 times smaller than the stretched chain.\nThe book solution quotes 281 nm and a contour length of 90 714 nm, obtained with 6.85 and 0.127 nm (projected bond length); the structure of the result (R of a few hundred nm against about 90 μm) is the same.","answers":["R ≈ 3.4×10² nm (about 340 nm)","Extended length ≈ 9×10⁴ nm (90 μm)","R/L ≈ 0.004, so the chain is strongly coiled"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 2.1"},{"id":"2.2","title":"Coil dimensions and Kuhn segments of polystyrene","q":"Polystyrene of molar mass 200 000 g/mol has a repeat unit of 104 g/mol with two backbone C-C bonds per repeat unit; the bond length is l = 0.154 nm and the characteristic ratio is C∞ = 9.7. Calculate (a) the mean squared and root-mean-square end-to-end distance, (b) the radius of gyration, (c) the contour length N l, and (d) the Kuhn segment length and number of Kuhn segments of the equivalent freely jointed chain.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: ⟨R²⟩ = C∞ N l²; ⟨Rg²⟩ = ⟨R²⟩/6; Kuhn chain from N l = N_k l_k and C∞ N l² = N_k l_k².\n2. Number of bonds: N = 2 × 200 000/104 = 3846.\n3. (a) ⟨R²⟩ = 9.7 × 3846 × 0.154² = 9.7 × 3846 × 0.02372 = 885 nm², so R = 29.7 nm.\n4. (b) Rg = R/√6 = 29.7/2.449 = 12.1 nm.\n5. (c) Contour length: L = N l = 3846 × 0.154 = 592 nm.\n6. (d) l_k = C∞ l = 9.7 × 0.154 = 1.49 nm; N_k = N/C∞ = 3846/9.7 = 397. Check: N_k l_k = 397 × 1.49 = 592 nm.\nThe coil (30 nm) is about 20 times shorter than the contour length.","answers":["⟨R²⟩ ≈ 885 nm², R ≈ 30 nm","Rg ≈ 12 nm","L ≈ 590 nm","l_k ≈ 1.5 nm, N_k ≈ 400"],"src":"course-style (lecture 9/7 example)"},{"id":"2.3","title":"Extended length and Gaussian size of a polyethylene molecule","q":"A linear polyethylene molecule has a molar mass of 84 000 g/mol. (a) Calculate the length of the fully extended planar zigzag chain, assuming C-C bonds of 0.154 nm and a fixed bond angle of 109.5°. (b) For fixed valence angle 109.5° and free rotation, a PE molecule with y C-C bonds is equivalent to a freely jointed chain of y/3 segments, each of the length of three zigzag bonds. Find the root-mean-square end-to-end distance R and the root-mean-square distance of the chain elements from the center of gravity.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: projected bond length b = l sin(τ/2); equivalent chain with N\u0027 = y/3 segments of length l\u0027 = 3b; then R = √N\u0027 l\u0027 and Rg = R/√6.\n2. Number of bonds: y = 84 000/14 = 6000.\n3. b = 0.154 × sin(54.75°) = 0.154 × 0.8165 = 0.1257 nm.\n4. (a) Extended length L = y b = 6000 × 0.1257 = 754 nm (the book gets 762 nm using 0.127 nm per bond).\n5. (b) N\u0027 = 2000 segments, l\u0027 = 3 × 0.1257 = 0.377 nm (N\u0027 l\u0027 = 754 nm preserved).\n6. R = √2000 × 0.377 = 44.7 × 0.377 = 16.9 nm.\n7. Rg = R/√6 = 16.9/2.449 = 6.9 nm (Debye relation ⟨Rg²⟩ = ⟨R²⟩/6).\nThe book answer key lists 17 nm and 2.84 nm; the second value equals R/6, whereas the Debye relation gives R/√6 = 6.9 nm.","answers":["Extended length ≈ 7.5×10² nm (about 755 nm)","R ≈ 17 nm","Rg ≈ 6.9 nm"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.14"},{"id":"2.4","title":"Gauche content and coil size of polyethylene at 150 °C","q":"Using the rotational isomeric state model with only first-order (independent bond) interactions, take the energy of a gauche state relative to trans as 2.1 kJ/mol, with two equivalent gauche states (g⁺ and g⁻) and a bond angle of 109.5°. At 150 °C (423 K) calculate (a) the statistical weight of a gauche state, (b) the fraction of bonds in gauche states, and (c) the ratio ⟨R²⟩/(N l²) of the hindered-rotation chain. Comment on the result compared with the measured value of about 6.7.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: σ = exp(-Eg/RT), trans fraction 1/(1+2σ), gauche fractions σ/(1+2σ) each; ⟨R²⟩/(N l²) = [(1+cos(180°-τ))/(1-cos(180°-τ))] [(2+σ)/(3σ)].\n2. (a) σ = exp(-2100/(8.314 × 423)) = exp(-0.597) = 0.55.\n3. (b) Gauche fraction = 2σ/(1+2σ) = 1.10/2.10 = 0.524, trans = 0.476.\n4. (c) cos(180°-109.5°) = cos 70.5° = 0.334, so the FRC factor is (1.334)/(0.666) = 2.00; hindrance factor (2+0.55)/(3 × 0.55) = 1.544.\n5. ⟨R²⟩/(N l²) = 2.00 × 1.544 = 3.1.\n6. First-order interactions alone give about 3, roughly half of the experimental 6.7; Flory showed that second-order interactions (strong penalty for g⁺g⁻ pairs, about 12 kJ/mol) are needed to reach 6.7-6.9.","answers":["σ ≈ 0.55","Gauche fraction ≈ 52 % (trans ≈ 48 %)","⟨R²⟩/(N l²) ≈ 3.1, below the measured 6.7 because g⁺g⁻ interactions are missing"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.16, Gedde Exercise 2.5"},{"id":"2.5","title":"Counting conformations of alkane chains","q":"Using the three rotational states trans, gauche⁺ and gauche⁻ for every inner C-C bond (rotational isomeric state counting, no energy weighting), count the distinguishable conformations of (a) n-pentane and (b) n-hexane, noting that a conformation read from the other end of the chain is the same molecule. (c) Estimate the number of conformations of a linear polyethylene molecule of molar mass 70 000 g/mol, ignoring symmetry, and express it as a power of ten.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: a chain of n carbons has n-3 inner rotatable bonds, each with 3 states, so 3^(n-3) sequences; reversal of the chain identifies sequence (a,b,...) with its reverse.\n2. (a) n-pentane: n-3 = 2 bonds, 9 sequences, 3 palindromes (TT, g⁺g⁺, g⁻g⁻ are their own reverses); distinguishable = (9+3)/2 = 6 (TT, Tg⁺, Tg⁻, g⁺g⁺, g⁺g⁻, g⁻g⁻).\n3. (b) n-hexane: 3 bonds, 27 sequences; palindromes have first = third, 3 × 3 = 9; distinguishable = (27+9)/2 = 18.\n4. (c) M = 70 000 g/mol gives n = 70 000/14 = 5000 carbons; number of conformations ≈ 3^(n-3) = 3^4997.\n5. Take log₁₀: 4997 × 0.4771 = 2384, so Ω ≈ 10^2384.\n6. In reality high-energy states (for example g⁺g⁻) have negligible weight, so the true accessible number is smaller, but still astronomically large.","answers":["n-pentane: 6","n-hexane: 18","PE of 70 000 g/mol: 3^4997 ≈ 10^2384"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.15, Gedde Exercise 2.2"},{"id":"2.6","title":"Entropic spring constant and force of a single chain","q":"Model a polymer chain as a Gaussian (freely jointed Kuhn) chain of N = 300 segments, each of length l = 1.0 nm, in a melt at 300 K. (a) Find the entropic spring constant. (b) Find the restoring force when the end-to-end distance is held at r = 20 nm. (c) Find the free-energy increase in units of k_BT. (d) By what factor does the force at the same r change if the temperature is raised to 360 K and the chain length is doubled? Take k_B = 1.381×10⁻²³ J/K.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: U = 0, so F = -TS = (3k_BT/2) r²/(N l²); force f = dF/dr = k_s r with k_s = 3k_BT/(N l²).\n2. k_BT = 1.381×10⁻²³ × 300 = 4.14×10⁻²¹ J.\n3. (a) N l² = 300 × (10⁻⁹ m)² = 3×10⁻¹⁶ m², so k_s = 3 × 4.14×10⁻²¹ / 3×10⁻¹⁶ = 4.1×10⁻⁵ N/m.\n4. (b) f = k_s r = 4.1×10⁻⁵ × 20×10⁻⁹ = 8.3×10⁻¹³ N ≈ 0.83 pN.\n5. (c) ΔF = (3/2) k_BT r²/(N l²) = 1.5 × 400/300 k_BT = 2.0 k_BT (8.3×10⁻²¹ J).\n6. (d) k_s ∝ T/N: factor (360/300)/2 = 0.6, so f = 0.50 pN. Heating stiffens the spring; a longer chain is softer.\nGaussian statistics is valid here since r = 20 nm is far below the contour length 300 nm.","answers":["k_s ≈ 4.1×10⁻⁵ N/m","f ≈ 0.83 pN","ΔF ≈ 2.0 k_BT","Force falls to about 0.50 pN (factor 0.6)"],"src":"course-style (lecture 9/9)"},{"id":"2.7","title":"Most probable and mean distances of a short random-walk chain","q":"A freely jointed chain has N = 9 segments of length l = 1 nm, so ⟨R²⟩ = N l². Using the Gaussian radial distribution P(r) = 4πr²(3/2π⟨R²⟩)^(3/2) exp(-3r²/2⟨R²⟩), find (a) the most probable end-to-end distance, (b) the mean end-to-end distance, and (c) the probability that the Gaussian distribution assigns to r larger than the contour length Nl, which in reality is zero. Comment on the validity of the Gaussian model.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: R = √⟨R²⟩ = √N l = 3 nm. For the radial density d/dr[r² exp(-3r²/2R²)] = 0 gives r² = (2/3)R².\n2. (a) r_mp = √(2/3) R = 0.816 × 3 = 2.45 nm.\n3. (b) ⟨r⟩ = √(8/(3π)) R = 0.921 × 3 = 2.76 nm (rms value 3.0 nm; the three values differ by a few tens of percent).\n4. (c) Contour length L = N l = 9 nm. P(r\u003eL) = erfc(a) + (2a/√π) e^(-a²), with a = L√(3/2)/R = 3.67, so a² = 13.5; erfc(3.67) ≈ 2×10⁻⁷ and the second term is 5.7×10⁻⁶, so P(r\u003eL) ≈ 6×10⁻⁶ (confirmed by numerical integration, 5.9×10⁻⁶).\n5. The probability is tiny but nonzero while the physical value is exactly zero. The Gaussian distribution is accurate for r ≪ L but fails near full extension, and the short chain (N = 9) is already at the limit; for N ≈ 100 and stretched chains non-Gaussian (Langevin) statistics are needed.","answers":["r_mp ≈ 2.45 nm","⟨r⟩ ≈ 2.76 nm","P(r \u003e Nl) ≈ 6×10⁻⁶ (unphysical but small)","Gaussian is valid only far from full extension"],"src":"course-style (lecture 9/9)"},{"id":"2.8","title":"Flory theorem and scaling of coil size","q":"(a) State the Flory theorem and explain why ideal-chain statistics apply to a polymer melt even though segments of a chain cannot overlap. (b) A polystyrene sample has its molar mass increased from 50 000 to 200 000 g/mol. By what factor does the rms end-to-end distance grow in a theta solvent (or in the melt) and in a good solvent, where R ∝ N^(3/5)? (c) How does the coil change on moving from a good solvent to a poor solvent, and what is special at the theta temperature?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Flory theorem: a chain in the melt has the unperturbed dimensions ⟨R²⟩0 = C∞ N l², the same as in a theta solvent. Argument (de Gennes): the excluded-volume repulsion that would swell a chain is canceled by the equal and opposite attraction from the surrounding identical chains, so a chain feels no net force and behaves as a phantom chain. Small-angle neutron scattering of labeled chains confirms it, also in crosslinked rubber.\n2. (b) The molar mass ratio is 200 000/50 000 = 4, so N rises by 4. Theta or melt: R ∝ N^(1/2), factor 4^(1/2) = 2.0. Good solvent: R ∝ N^(3/5), factor 4^(0.6) = 2.30 (experimental exponent about 0.59, factor 2.27).\n3. (c) The expansion factor is α \u003e 1 in a good solvent, α \u003c 1 in a poor solvent. At the theta temperature, solvent-polymer and polymer-polymer effects balance (α = 1): the long-range repulsion is exactly compensated, the second virial coefficient is zero and the chain is ideal.","answers":["Flory theorem: chains in a melt are unperturbed (theta-like), R² = C∞ N l²","Theta/melt: ×2.0; good solvent: ×2.3","α \u003e 1 good, α = 1 theta (ideal), α \u003c 1 poor solvent"],"src":"Gedde §2.3 (course-style)"},{"id":"3.1","title":"Limiting glass transition temperature of polystyrene fractions","q":"Number-average molar mass M and glass transition temperature Tg were measured for seven narrow fractions of atactic polystyrene:\nM (g/mol): 500, 1000, 4000, 5000, 6000, 10000, 20000\nTg (K): 182, 278, 354, 361, 362, 369.5, 375.5\nAssuming the molar mass dependence Tg = Tg∞ − K/M, determine Tg∞ for infinite molar mass and the constant K. Then estimate Tg for a fraction with M = 3000 g/mol.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the free-volume (chain-end) relation Tg = Tg∞ − K/M is linear in 1/M, so fit Tg against 1/M and read the intercept.\n2. Set-up: x = 1/M (mol/g): 2.0e-3, 1.0e-3, 2.5e-4, 2.0e-4, 1.667e-4, 1.0e-4, 5.0e-5.\n3. Least-squares straight line Tg = a + b x over the seven points gives slope b = −9.92e4 K g/mol and intercept a = 379.4 K.\n4. Hence Tg∞ = a = 379.4 K (106 °C) and K = −b = 9.92e4 K g/mol.\n5. Check: M = 10000 gives 379.4 − 9.92 = 369.5 K, equal to the measured value.\n6. For M = 3000 g/mol: Tg = 379.4 − 9.92e4/3000 = 379.4 − 33.1 = 346.3 K (about 73 °C).\n7. Remark: the lowest-M points (500 g/mol) deviate somewhat because 1/M is large and the linear form is only an approximation.","answers":["Tg∞ ≈ 379 K (≈106 °C)","K ≈ 9.9e4 K·g/mol","Tg(3000 g/mol) ≈ 346 K (≈73 °C)"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 5.3"},{"id":"3.2","title":"Tg of a non-crystalline ethylene-propylene random copolymer","q":"A random ethylene-propylene copolymer contains 20 CH3 side groups per 100 main-chain carbon atoms (each propylene unit contributes one CH3 and two main-chain carbons; each ethylene unit two main-chain carbons). The relative molar masses are ethylene unit 28 and propylene unit 42. The homopolymers have Tg = −120 °C (polyethylene) and −19 °C (polypropylene). (a) Find the weight fraction of propylene. (b) Estimate the copolymer Tg with the Fox equation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: convert the CH3 count to a composition, then use the Fox (inverse-average) rule for a miscible/random system.\n2. 100 main-chain carbons = 50 monomer units. 20 CH3 groups means 20 propylene units and 30 ethylene units.\n3. Masses per 100 carbons: propylene 20 × 42 = 840; ethylene 30 × 28 = 840. Total 1680.\n4. Weight fraction of propylene w2 = 840/1680 = 0.50; ethylene w1 = 0.50.\n5. Temperatures in kelvin: Tg1 = 153.15 K, Tg2 = 254.15 K.\n6. Fox: 1/Tg = w1/Tg1 + w2/Tg2 = 0.5/153.15 + 0.5/254.15 = 3.265e-3 + 1.967e-3 = 5.232e-3 K^-1.\n7. Tg = 191.1 K = −82 °C.\n8. Remark: this composition is just non-crystalline (maximum crystallinity 0 %), so the copolymer is a fully amorphous rubber with Tg between the homopolymer values.","answers":["Propylene weight fraction 0.50","Tg ≈ 191 K ≈ −82 °C"],"src":"McCrum Problems 2.10 and 2.11"},{"id":"3.3","title":"Opacity and glass transitions in two polymer blends","q":"Blend A shows a single glass transition but is opaque. Blend B shows two glass transitions but is transparent. For each blend give a physical explanation, state what each observation (Tg and optical clarity) normally indicates about miscibility, and propose an experiment that would resolve the ambiguity.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Concept: a single Tg normally indicates a miscible (molecularly mixed) blend, two Tgs indicate two phases, and opacity indicates light scattering from domains comparable to or larger than the wavelength with a refractive-index mismatch.\n2. Blend A (one Tg, opaque): the components are immiscible (opaque means scattering domains), but their Tg values are so close that the two steps merge into one.\n3. Blend B (two Tg, transparent): the components are immiscible (two Tgs). Transparency arises either because the refractive indices of the two polymers nearly match, or because the dispersed domains are much smaller than the wavelength of light (finely dispersed morphology).\n4. Resolving experiments: dynamic mechanical or DSC on blends of different composition (Tg shift following Fox indicates miscibility); electron or atomic-force microscopy and small-angle scattering to see domain size; measure refractive index of the components; change Tg of one component (e.g. by plasticising) to separate the transitions.","answers":["A: immiscible, Tgs coincide, domains scatter light","B: immiscible, matched refractive index or domain size \u003c\u003c wavelength","Resolve by microscopy/SAXS, composition-dependent Tg, refractive index"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 5.4"},{"id":"3.4","title":"Melt viscosity of polystyrene just above its glass transition","q":"Polystyrene (Tg = 100 °C) has a viscosity of 1e12 Pa·s at its glass transition temperature. Using the WLF equation with its universal constants (C1 = 17.44, C2 = 51.6 K, referenced to Tg), estimate (a) the viscosity at 120 °C, (b) the factor by which the viscosity falls between 100 °C and 130 °C, and (c) the fractional free volume at Tg and the thermal expansion coefficient of the free volume implied by these constants (take B = 1).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: universal WLF with reference temperature Tg: log aT = −C1 (T − Tg)/(C2 + T − Tg), where aT = η(T)/η(Tg).\n2. At 120 °C: T − Tg = 20 K, log aT = −17.44 × 20/(51.6 + 20) = −348.8/71.6 = −4.87.\n3. η(120 °C) = 1e12 × 10^−4.87 = 1.3e7 Pa·s.\n4. At 130 °C: T − Tg = 30 K, log aT = −17.44 × 30/81.6 = −6.41, so aT = 3.9e−7 and η = 3.9e5 Pa·s. The fall from 100 to 130 °C is a factor of 1/aT ≈ 2.6e6.\n5. Free volume: C1 = B/(2.303 fg) so fg = 1/(2.303 × 17.44) = 0.025 (2.5 %).\n6. C2 = fg/αf so αf = 0.025/51.6 K = 4.8e−4 K^-1.\n7. Remark: WLF is valid only from Tg to about Tg + 100 K.","answers":["η(120 °C) ≈ 1.3e7 Pa·s","η drops by ≈ 2.6e6 between 100 and 130 °C (η ≈ 3.9e5 Pa·s)","fg ≈ 0.025, αf ≈ 4.8e−4 1/K"],"src":"course-style (Gedde §5.4)"},{"id":"3.5","title":"Scaling of melt flow with molar mass and shear thinning","q":"A polyethylene melt (entanglement-controlled regime, M well above Mc) is extruded through a capillary. (a) The molar mass is doubled at the same temperature. By what factor does the zero-shear viscosity change, and what factor would reptation theory (exponent 3) give? (b) The melt is a power-law fluid with n = 0.4. If the pressure drop across the capillary is doubled, by what factor does the volume flow rate change, compared with a Newtonian liquid? (c) Why is the lecture\u0027s plug-flow statement consistent with a small n?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Above Mc, η0 ∝ M^3.4. Doubling M multiplies η0 by 2^3.4 = 10.6. Reptation (η0 ∝ M^3) predicts 2^3 = 8. The measured exponent is larger than 3 because of other relaxation routes (tube renewal, constraint release).\n2. (b) Capillary flow rate: Q ∝ (ΔP/K)^(1/n) R^((3n+1)/n)/L^(1/n). For fixed geometry Q ∝ ΔP^(1/n) = ΔP^2.5.\n3. Doubling ΔP: Q increases by 2^2.5 = 5.66. A Newtonian liquid (n = 1, Hagen-Poiseuille, Q ∝ ΔP) would only double.\n4. (c) For a power-law fluid the velocity profile is v(r) ∝ R^((n+1)/n) − r^((n+1)/n); the exponent (n+1)/n = 3.5 here is large, so v is nearly uniform over most of the radius (plug flow) with a steep gradient near the wall. The smaller n, the flatter the profile; the Newtonian parabola corresponds to exponent 2.\n5. Physically, shear thinning concentrates shear at the wall, where the high rate has destroyed entanglements.","answers":["Viscosity × 10.6 (reptation: × 8)","Flow rate × 5.7 (Newtonian: × 2)","Small n gives an exponent (n+1)/n = 3.5, so a flat plug profile"],"src":"course-style (Gedde §6.2-6.3)"},{"id":"3.6","title":"Cooling-rate dependence of Tg and physical ageing","q":"A DSC scan on cooling a glassy polymer at 10 K/min gives Tg = 105 °C. (a) Using the rule that Tg changes by about 3 K per decade of cooling rate, estimate Tg for a slow cooling at 0.1 K/min. (b) The sample is quenched to 25 °C and stored for six months. Describe qualitatively how volume, enthalpy, modulus, yield stress and impact strength change, and why. (c) Outline an experiment to record this ageing and a treatment that erases it.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Cooling rate falls from 10 to 0.1 K/min: two decades. Tg decreases by about 2 × 3 K = 6 K, so Tg ≈ 99 °C. Slower cooling lets the structure keep up with equilibrium to lower temperature.\n2. (b) A quenched glass is frozen with excess free volume and enthalpy (above the equilibrium line). Since polymers retain segmental (subglass) mobility, free volume decreases slowly with time (physical ageing, logarithmic-like in time, self-retarding because mobility falls as free volume shrinks).\n3. Consequences: specific volume and enthalpy decrease; modulus and yield stress increase; fracture toughness, impact strength, permeability and creep compliance decrease; the material becomes stiffer and more brittle.\n4. (c) Experiment: heat above Tg to erase history, cool at constant rate to T2 below Tg, then hold isothermally while recording volume (dilatometer) or enthalpy (DSC, compare the reheating enthalpy peak of aged and unaged samples).\n5. Erasing: heat above Tg (rejuvenation), then quench again.","answers":["Tg ≈ 99 °C (≈ −6 K)","Volume, enthalpy down; modulus, yield stress up; toughness down","Dilatometry/DSC isothermal recovery; reheating above Tg erases it"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 5.2 and lecture 9/16"},{"id":"3.7","title":"Brittle fracture stress of a glassy polymer with a flaw","q":"A glassy polymer plate has Young\u0027s modulus E = 3.0 GPa and contains an internal crack of half-length a = 50 µm. (a) Using Griffith\u0027s criterion for plane stress, σf = sqrt(E·Gc/(π a)), calculate the fracture stress if Gc = 400 J/m² (measured for a craze-toughened glass). (b) Repeat using the surface-energy estimate Gc = 2γ with γ = 0.03 J/m². (c) Explain the difference and why crazing is involved.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Griffith balance of elastic energy release against fracture energy for a crack of half-length a: σf = sqrt(E Gc/(π a)).\n2. (a) E Gc = 3.0e9 × 400 = 1.2e12 Pa·J/m². π a = π × 50e−6 = 1.571e−4 m.\n3. σf = sqrt(1.2e12/1.571e−4) = sqrt(7.64e15) = 8.7e7 Pa = 87 MPa.\n4. (b) Gc = 2γ = 0.06 J/m²: E Gc = 1.8e8; σf = sqrt(1.8e8/1.571e−4) = sqrt(1.146e12) = 1.07e6 Pa ≈ 1.1 MPa.\n5. (c) The pure surface-energy (bond scission) estimate is about 400/0.06 ≈ 7000 times smaller in Gc and 80 times smaller in stress than the measured value. The real Gc is large because a craze (voided fibrillar zone) develops ahead of the crack tip and dissipates energy by plastic work and chain pull-out, so the crack grows only after much more energy has been spent.\n6. Remark: stress at the tip of a hole is concentrated up to about three times the applied stress for a circular hole; sharp cracks concentrate far more.","answers":["σf ≈ 87 MPa with Gc = 400 J/m²","σf ≈ 1.1 MPa with Gc = 2γ","Crazing (and plastic work) raises Gc by orders of magnitude"],"src":"course-style (lecture 9/21)"},{"id":"3.8","title":"Cohesive energy density and solubility parameter of polystyrene","q":"The molar enthalpy of vaporisation of ethylbenzene (a low-molar-mass analogue of the polystyrene repeat unit) is ΔHvap = 42.3 kJ/mol at 298 K and its molar volume is 123 cm³/mol. (a) Calculate the cohesive energy density CED and solubility parameter δ = sqrt(CED). (b) Why is the monomer styrene not used as the analogue? (c) State two properties that correlate with CED and one class of polymers for which the correlation fails.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: cohesive energy density is the energy per unit volume needed to remove all secondary bonds, i.e. the vaporisation enthalpy minus the expansion work RT, divided by the molar volume.\n2. Expansion work: RT = 8.314 × 298 = 2.48 kJ/mol.\n3. CED = (42.3 − 2.48) kJ/mol / (123e−6 m³/mol) = 39.8e3/123e−6 = 3.24e8 J/m³ = 324 MPa.\n4. δ = sqrt(324 MPa) = 18.0 MPa^(1/2) (= 18 (J/cm³)^(1/2)).\n5. (b) Styrene has a vinyl double bond that is absent in the polymer, so its cohesive interactions are not representative; ethylbenzene is saturated like the repeat unit.\n6. (c) Young\u0027s modulus and Tg increase with CED in amorphous polymers (stronger secondary bonds, more rigid glass). The correlation fails for semicrystalline polymers where crystal structure and morphology dominate.","answers":["CED ≈ 324 MPa (3.2e8 J/m³)","δ ≈ 18 MPa^½","Styrene has a C=C absent in the polymer","Modulus and Tg correlate; fails for semicrystalline polymers"],"src":"course-style (lecture 9/16)"},{"id":"4.1","title":"Melting points of polyethylene crystals of different thickness","q":"Calculate the melting points of linear polyethylene crystals with thickness 100 nm, 50 nm, 10 nm and 5 nm. Assume the Thomson-Gibbs equation is valid and use: heat of fusion Δh0 = 293 J/g, crystal density ρc = 1000 kg/m³, equilibrium melting point Tm0 = 415 K, fold surface free energy σe = 90 mJ/m². Comment on what the result implies for the crystal thickness distribution and the melting range.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the Thomson-Gibbs relation Tm = Tm0 [1 − 2σe/(Δh0 ρc Lc)] accounts for the extra free energy of the fold surfaces.\n2. Convert Δh0 to J/kg: 293 J/g = 2.93e5 J/kg. Then Δh0 ρc = 2.93e5 × 1000 = 2.93e8 J/m³.\n3. Coefficient: 2σe/(Δh0 ρc) = 2 × 0.090/2.93e8 = 6.14e-10 m.\n4. Lc = 100 nm: 6.14e-10/1.0e-7 = 0.00614, Tm = 415 (1 − 0.00614) = 412.5 K.\n5. Lc = 50 nm: 0.01229, Tm = 409.9 K.\n6. Lc = 10 nm: 0.0614, Tm = 389.5 K.\n7. Lc = 5 nm: 0.1229, Tm = 364.0 K.\n8. Comment: Tm falls steeply for thicknesses below about 20 nm. A sample with a lamellar thickness distribution therefore melts over a range (here about 50 K between 5 and 50 nm crystals), and annealing, which thickens lamellae, raises the melting point.","answers":["Lc = 100 nm: Tm ≈ 412.5 K","Lc = 50 nm: ≈ 409.9 K","Lc = 10 nm: ≈ 389.5 K (390 K)","Lc = 5 nm: ≈ 364 K"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 7.2"},{"id":"4.2","title":"Crystal density from unit-cell dimensions","q":"Calculate the density of (a) the orthorhombic polyethylene crystal with unit-cell edges a = 736 pm, b = 492 pm, c = 254 pm (chain axis) containing 2 −CH2−CH2− units, and (b) the monoclinic α form of isotactic polypropylene with a = 666 pm, b = 2078 pm, c = 650 pm (chain axis), β = 99.6° (α = γ = 90°), containing 12 −CH2−CH(CH3)− units per cell. Use relative atomic masses H = 1, C = 12 and Avogadro\u0027s constant 6.023e23 mol⁻¹.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: ρc = Z M0/(NA V), where Z is the number of repeat units per cell, M0 their molar mass and V the cell volume.\n2. (a) PE: M0 = 28 g/mol (C2H4). Mass in cell = 2 × 28e-3/6.023e23 = 9.30e-26 kg.\n3. Orthorhombic volume V = a b c = 0.736 × 0.492 × 0.254 nm³ = 0.0920 nm³ = 9.198e-29 m³.\n4. ρ = 9.30e-26/9.198e-29 = 1011 kg/m³.\n5. (b) iPP: M0 = 42 g/mol (C3H6). Mass in cell = 12 × 42e-3/6.023e23 = 8.37e-25 kg.\n6. Monoclinic volume V = a b c sin β = 0.666 × 2.078 × 0.650 × sin 99.6° nm³ = 0.8996 × 0.9863 = 0.8872 nm³ = 8.872e-28 m³.\n7. ρ = 8.37e-25/8.872e-28 = 943 kg/m³.\n8. Remark: both are above the amorphous densities (about 855 and 855-860 kg/m³), consistent with denser packing in the crystal; the crystal density is the upper limit when evaluating crystallinity from density.","answers":["PE crystal ≈ 1011 kg/m³","iPP α crystal ≈ 943 kg/m³"],"src":"McCrum Problems 2.1 and 2.2"},{"id":"4.3","title":"Mass crystallinity from sample density","q":"(a) At 20 °C the specific volume of crystalline polyethylene is vc = 0.989e-3 m³/kg and that of amorphous polyethylene (extrapolated liquid) is va = 1.16e-3 m³/kg. Calculate the mass crystallinity of samples with densities 926, 940 and 955 kg/m³. (b) Polyoxymethylene (POM) has crystal and amorphous densities of 1506 and 1250 kg/m³; what is the mass crystallinity of a sample of density 1410 kg/m³?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the specific volume is the mass-weighted sum v = x vc + (1 − x) va, so x = (va − v)/(va − vc).\n2. va − vc = 1.16e-3 − 0.989e-3 = 0.171e-3 m³/kg.\n3. (a) ρ = 926: v = 1.0799e-3; x = (1.16 − 1.0799)/0.171 = 0.468.\n4. ρ = 940: v = 1.0638e-3; x = 0.0962/0.171 = 0.563.\n5. ρ = 955: v = 1.0471e-3; x = 0.1129/0.171 = 0.660.\n6. (b) POM, same relation written with densities: x = ρc (ρ − ρa)/(ρ (ρc − ρa)) = 1506 × (1410 − 1250)/(1410 × (1506 − 1250)) = 1506 × 160/(1410 × 256) = 240960/360960 = 0.668.\n7. Remark: this assumes a sharp two-phase model with known phase densities; the method is not usable when va ≈ vc or for PTFE with voids.","answers":["PE: 47 %, 56 %, 66 %","POM: 67 %"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.7"},{"id":"4.4","title":"Long period and crystal thickness from small-angle X-ray scattering","q":"A medium-density polyethylene shows a maximum in its SAXS intensity at a scattering angle 2θ = 0.43° (Cu radiation, λ = 0.15 nm). The mass crystallinity is wc = 0.60, with crystal density ρc = 1000 kg/m³ and amorphous density ρa = 855 kg/m³. Assuming a perfect alternating lamellar stack, calculate (a) the long period L, (b) the volume crystallinity, and (c) the average crystal thickness Lc and amorphous layer thickness La.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: SAXS from a lamellar stack obeys the Bragg condition λ = 2 L sin θ for the first-order peak, where 2θ is the scattering angle; the long period is L = Lc + La.\n2. θ = 0.215° = 3.75e-3 rad, sin θ = 3.75e-3.\n3. L = λ/(2 sin θ) = 0.15 nm/(2 × 3.75e-3) = 20.0 nm.\n4. Volume crystallinity vc = (wc/ρc)/(wc/ρc + (1 − wc)/ρa) = (0.60/1000)/(0.60/1000 + 0.40/855) = 6.0e-4/(6.0e-4 + 4.68e-4) = 0.562.\n5. Lc = vc L = 0.562 × 20.0 = 11.2 nm.\n6. La = L − Lc = 8.8 nm.\n7. Remark: this is a linear-crystallinity estimate; if the stack is not perfectly periodic, the value is an average, and the real interface is diffuse.","answers":["L ≈ 20 nm","Volume crystallinity ≈ 56 %","Lc ≈ 11 nm, La ≈ 9 nm"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 7.11 (modified)"},{"id":"4.5","title":"Avrami analysis of isothermal DSC crystallisation data","q":"A highly branched polyethylene was crystallised isothermally at 110 °C. The cumulative heat evolved, Δh(t), was recorded by DSC:\nt (s): 30, 60, 90, 120\nΔh (J/g): 1.9, 7.5, 14, 17\nThe heat evolved at the end of primary crystallisation is Δh∞ = 24 J/g. Take the crystallinity fraction as X = Δh/Δh∞ and assume the Avrami equation 1 − X = exp(−K tⁿ) applies at low conversion. (a) Determine n and K from a linear fit. (b) Calculate the half-crystallisation time. (c) What does n imply about the nucleation and growth geometry?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: linearise: ln[−ln(1 − X)] = ln K + n ln t and fit a line.\n2. X values: 0.0792, 0.3125, 0.5833, 0.7083 for t = 30, 60, 90, 120 s.\n3. y = ln[−ln(1 − X)]: −2.50, −0.99, −0.13, +0.21 (approximately). x = ln t: 3.40, 4.09, 4.50, 4.79.\n4. Least-squares fit through the four points: slope n ≈ 2.0, intercept ln K ≈ −9.23, so K ≈ 1.0e-4 s^-2.\n5. Half-time: t1/2 = (ln 2/K)^(1/n) = (0.693/9.8e-5)^(1/2.0) = (7.1e3)^(0.5) ≈ 85 s.\n6. Interpretation: n ≈ 2 suggests low-dimensional growth, e.g. athermal (instantaneous) nucleation with circular (disc, 2D) lamellar growth, or thermal nucleation of rod-like growth; spherulitic 3D growth with constant-rate nucleation would give 4. For a branched PE with slow, incomplete crystallisation the low value is plausible.\n7. Remark: the fit is valid only at low conversion; later points deviate because of impingement and secondary crystallisation.","answers":["n ≈ 2.0","K ≈ 1e-4 s^-2","t1/2 ≈ 85 s","n = 2 indicates 2D (disc-like) or low-dimensional growth"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 8.4"},{"id":"4.6","title":"Nucleation constant from linear growth-rate data","q":"The linear growth rate G of spherulites of a branched polyethylene was measured by polarised light microscopy:\nTc (K): 379.2, 380.2, 381.2, 382.2, 383.1, 383.1, 384.1, 384.2, 385.6, 386.2, 387.2, 388.3, 389.3, 390.2, 391.1, 392.3, 393.2\nG (µm/s): 0.51, 0.56, 0.50, 0.404, 0.234, 0.244, 0.206, 0.172, 0.0919, 0.070, 0.0298, 0.0278, 0.0154, 0.00926, 0.0062, 0.0029, 0.000777\nThe equilibrium melting point is Tm0 = 410 K. Using the Lauritzen-Hoffman equation G = G0 exp(−Kg/(Tc ΔT)) (neglect the transport term and the factor f), determine Kg from a plot of log10 G against 1/(Tc ΔT). Then, assuming regime III kinetics (Kg = 4bσσe Tm0/(Δh k)), what is the corresponding Kg for regime II growth of the same polymer?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: ln G = ln G0 − Kg/(Tc ΔT), so a plot of log10 G against x = 1/(Tc ΔT), ΔT = Tm0 − Tc, has slope −Kg/2.303.\n2. Compute x for each point, e.g. Tc = 379.2 K: ΔT = 30.8 K, x = 1/(379.2 × 30.8) = 8.56e-5 K⁻²; Tc = 393.2 K: ΔT = 16.8 K, x = 1.514e-4 K⁻².\n3. log10 G values range from −0.29 (Tc = 379.2 K) to −3.11 (393.2 K).\n4. Linear least-squares fit over all 17 points: log10 G = 3.58 − 43 340 x (slope −4.33e4 K²).\n5. Kg = 2.303 × 4.334e4 K² ≈ 1.0e5 K².\n6. LH theory: Kg(regime I) = Kg(regime III) = 4bσσeTm0/(Δh k) and Kg(II) = 2bσσeTm0/(Δh k), so Kg(II) = Kg/2 ≈ 5.0e4 K² if the fitted value is regime III (or I).\n7. Remark: the curvature or slope break in the plot would identify regime changes; here a single line was fitted for simplicity.","answers":["Kg ≈ 1.0e5 K² (slope ≈ −4.3e4 K² in the log10 plot)","Regime II Kg ≈ 5e4 K²"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 8.10"},{"id":"4.7","title":"Shortening the moulding cycle of isotactic polypropylene","q":"The solidification time of an iPP part is a significant fraction of the injection-moulding cycle time. (a) Sketch how the overall crystallisation rate depends on temperature between Tg (about −10 °C) and Tm0 (about 187 °C) and indicate where the mould surface and the melt interior should be controlled. (b) Propose three actions that shorten solidification, with the molecular or kinetic reasoning for each. (c) State two property consequences of your choices.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) The overall rate is the product of a diffusion factor (zero at Tg, increasing with T) and a nucleation factor (zero at Tm0, increasing with supercooling), giving a bell curve with a maximum roughly 0.2 Tm (K) below Tm, i.e. about 90-110 °C for iPP. Cycle time is minimised by holding the mould near the rate maximum rather than cold-quenching (nucleation-limited at the high-T side, diffusion-limited at the low-T side).\n2. (b) Action 1: add a nucleating agent (e.g. sodium benzoate, sorbitol derivative): heterogeneous nucleation lowers the barrier ΔG* and raises the nucleation density, so spherulites impinge sooner and the Avrami half-time falls. Action 2: choose the mould temperature near the maximum rate so growth rate G is high. Action 3: use a grade with narrower, lower-entanglement molar mass or higher stereoregularity, or thinner walls / better cooling to remove heat faster.\n3. (c) Consequences: smaller, more numerous spherulites improve clarity and toughness; higher crystallinity raises stiffness and shrinkage/warpage; too cold a mould gives low crystallinity and later secondary crystallisation (post-moulding shrinkage).","answers":["Bell-shaped rate; hold mould near the maximum (about 0.2 Tm below Tm)","Nucleating agent, optimised mould temperature, thin walls/faster heat removal","Smaller spherulites, higher stiffness, possible shrinkage/warpage"],"src":"Gedde Exercises 8.1 and 8.2"},{"id":"5.1","title":"Predicting isotropisation temperature trends from mesogen structure","q":"A main-chain thermotropic polyester has a mesogen consisting of two para-linked phenylene rings joined by an ester group, separated by an oligomethylene spacer of n = 6 CH2 units. Predict, with reasons, whether the nematic-isotropic transition temperature Ti rises or falls for each modification: (a) adding a third para-linked phenylene ring to the mesogen; (b) replacing one p-phenylene ring by m-phenylene; (c) adding a methyl or chlorine substituent on a ring (lateral); (d) increasing the spacer from n = 6 to n = 7, and from n = 7 to n = 8.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the isotropisation transition is first order, Ti = ΔHi/ΔSi, and is controlled mainly by the shape (aspect ratio and linearity) of the mesogen rather than by polarity.\n2. (a) A longer linear mesogen has a larger length-to-width ratio, so the nematic phase is more stable: Ti rises (Gedde Table 6.3).\n3. (b) The meta linkage bends the rod, reducing linearity and the effective aspect ratio: Ti and the melting point fall markedly.\n4. (c) A lateral substituent increases the width of the mesogen, reducing the aspect ratio and disturbing packing; Ti falls. Cl (polar) and CH3 (non-polar) give almost the same Ti, showing shape dominates over polarity.\n5. (d) Flexible spacers dilute the rod content so Ti decreases with spacer length overall, but there is an odd-even effect: even n gives higher Ti and a larger ΔSi than odd n. Thus n = 7 (odd) has a lower Ti than n = 6 (even), and n = 8 (even) has a higher Ti than n = 7 although the overall trend with n is downward; longer spacers also tend to favour smectic order.","answers":["(a) rises (larger aspect ratio)","(b) falls strongly (loss of linearity)","(c) falls (wider mesogen; polarity unimportant)","(d) odd-even effect: Ti(6) \u003e Ti(7) \u003c Ti(8), overall decreasing with n"],"src":"Gedde §6.5.4 (cf. Exercise 6.3)"},{"id":"5.2","title":"Order parameter of nematic rod distributions","q":"The nematic order parameter is S = (3⟨cos²θ⟩ − 1)/2, where θ is the angle between a rod and the director. Calculate (a) S for a domain with ⟨cos²θ⟩ = 0.82; (b) the value of ⟨cos²θ⟩ for S = 0.3 and S = 0.8 (the typical range of nematic domains); (c) S for a hypothetical sample in which 70 % of the rods lie parallel to the director (θ = 0°) and 30 % perpendicular to it (θ = 90°); (d) S of an isotropic liquid, for which all orientations in three dimensions are equally likely.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: insert the averaged squared cosine into S = (3⟨cos²θ⟩ − 1)/2.\n2. (a) S = (3 × 0.82 − 1)/2 = (2.46 − 1)/2 = 0.73.\n3. (b) Invert: ⟨cos²θ⟩ = (2S + 1)/3. For S = 0.3: (0.6 + 1)/3 = 0.533. For S = 0.8: (1.6 + 1)/3 = 0.867.\n4. (c) ⟨cos²θ⟩ = 0.7 × cos²0° + 0.3 × cos²90° = 0.7 × 1 + 0 = 0.70. S = (3 × 0.70 − 1)/2 = 0.55.\n5. (d) For isotropic three-dimensional orientation ⟨cos²θ⟩ = 1/3, so S = (1 − 1)/2 = 0. Perfect alignment (θ = 0 for all rods) gives S = 1.\n6. Remark: S is the analogue of the Hermans orientation function and is typically 0.3-0.8 in a nematic domain and falls to zero at the isotropisation temperature.","answers":["(a) S = 0.73","(b) ⟨cos²θ⟩ = 0.533 (S = 0.3) and 0.867 (S = 0.8)","(c) S = 0.55","(d) S = 0"],"src":"Gedde §6.5.3 (cf. Exercise 6.7)"},{"id":"5.3","title":"Critical concentration for a nematic rigid-rod solution","q":"In Flory\u0027s lattice theory, rigid rods of aspect ratio x form an anisotropic phase above the critical volume fraction v2* = (8/x)(1 − 2/x). (a) Calculate v2* for x = 20, 50 and 100. (b) A semiflexible aramid has a Kuhn segment length of 60 nm and a molecular diameter of 0.6 nm. What is its effective aspect ratio and v2*? (c) A 20 wt% solution of this polymer (density 1.44 g/cm³) in sulphuric acid (1.84 g/cm³) is prepared: is it expected to be nematic? Assume ideal volume additivity.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: evaluate Flory\u0027s relation; for semiflexible chains the aspect ratio of the Kuhn segment replaces the rod length-to-width ratio.\n2. (a) x = 20: v2* = (8/20)(1 − 0.1) = 0.4 × 0.9 = 0.36. x = 50: (8/50)(1 − 0.04) = 0.16 × 0.96 = 0.154. x = 100: 0.08 × 0.98 = 0.078.\n3. (b) x = lK/d = 60/0.6 = 100, so v2* = 0.078 (about 7.8 vol%).\n4. (c) Volume of polymer in 100 g solution: 20/1.44 = 13.9 cm³; solvent 80/1.84 = 43.5 cm³. Volume fraction v2 = 13.9/(13.9 + 43.5) = 0.242.\n5. Since 0.242 is much larger than 0.078, the solution is above the critical concentration and is expected to be nematic (anisotropic), consistent with the use of concentrated sulphuric acid solutions for spinning Kevlar.\n6. Remark: the original Flory theory ignores interactions (athermal); poor solvents (χ12 \u003e 0.1) widen the biphasic region.","answers":["v2* = 0.36 (x=20), 0.154 (x=50), 0.078 (x=100)","x = 100, v2* ≈ 0.078","v2 = 0.24 \u003e v2*: nematic"],"src":"course-style (Gedde §6.5.6)"},{"id":"5.4","title":"Equilibration of oriented melts of three polymer types","q":"Elongational flow orients polymer melts. Discuss the mechanisms by which the orientation relaxes (or does not) for (a) a flexible-chain polymer of low molar mass, (b) a flexible-chain polymer of high molar mass, (c) a nematic liquid crystalline polymer. State what controls the relaxation time in each case and how it scales with molar mass where applicable.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: relate relaxation of the oriented melt to the mechanism of chain rearrangement and to the entanglement state.\n2. (a) Low M (below the critical entanglement mass Mc): few or no entanglements. Chains recoil to random coils by Rouse-type motion controlled by the monomeric friction coefficient; relaxation time τ ∝ M (and η0 ∝ M). Orientation relaxes rapidly.\n3. (b) High M (above Mc): numerous entanglements restrict each chain to a tube. Orientation relaxes by reptation (disentangling by snake-like motion along the tube) with τ ∝ M³ (observed about M^3.4) and η0 ∝ M^3.4. Relaxation is slow, which is the origin of frozen-in orientation, die swell and extrudate distortion.\n4. (c) Nematic LCP: the rod-like chains keep their extended shape (no coil recoil, no entropic driving force to randomise), and domains align along the flow. The relaxation of the aligned director field requires slow collective reorientation and annihilation of disclinations, so relaxation is extremely slow: orientation is retained after solidification, which gives high stiffness and strength in the flow direction.\n5. Summary: flexible low-M: fast, monomeric friction; flexible high-M: slow, reptation; LCP: very slow, shape intact.","answers":["Low M: fast, controlled by monomeric friction (τ ∝ M)","High M: slow reptation, τ ∝ M^3 to M^3.4","LCP: chains stay extended, very slow relaxation, orientation retained"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 6.1"},{"id":"5.5","title":"Molecular requirements of melt-processable liquid crystalline polymers","q":"List the requirements that a melt-processable liquid crystalline polymer must satisfy, and state what kind of molecular structure delivers the desired rheological and thermal properties. Illustrate with the example of the copolyester of p-hydroxybenzoic acid and 2,6-hydroxynaphthoic acid (Vectra), and explain why the fully rigid homopolymer of p-hydroxybenzoic acid cannot be melt processed.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: (i) the polymer must be nematic over an extensive temperature range; (ii) its melting temperature must be well below the isotropisation temperature (depressed melting point, so a broad nematic window); (iii) both transitions must lie below the degradation temperature so that it can be processed without decomposition; (iv) low melt viscosity and a stable oriented structure after cooling.\n2. Structure: statistical copolymers whose repeating units contain kinks, off-axis (crankshaft) or flexible units. They disrupt regular crystal packing, lowering Tm and crystallinity, without destroying the rod-like character that maintains nematic order.\n3. Vectra: the p-hydroxybenzoate units are linear rods; the 2,6-naphthalene units introduce lateral and longitudinal steps (a kink), producing random sequences. This lowers the crystal size and Tm, so the melt is processable and nematic.\n4. The homopolymer of p-hydroxybenzoic acid is fully linear and regular: it crystallises strongly, its melting temperature is very high (it decomposes before melting), so it cannot be processed in the melt.","answers":["Broad nematic range, Tm well below Ti, both below degradation","Statistical copolymers with kinks or flexible units","Naphthalene comonomer in Vectra disrupts packing, lowering Tm"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 6.5"},{"id":"5.6","title":"Melting temperature from enthalpy and entropy of fusion","q":"Polyethylene has a heat of fusion of 293 J/g and an equilibrium melting temperature of 414 K (repeat unit CH2, molar mass 14.03 g/mol). (a) Calculate the entropy of fusion per mole of CH2. (b) Hypothetical polymers have: A (flexible chain), Δh = 8.0 kJ/mol and Δs = 17 J/(mol K); B (same interactions as A but a rigid chain which is already extended in the melt), Δh = 8.0 kJ/mol and Δs = 8.0 J/(mol K); C (flexible chain with hydrogen bonds), Δh = 16.0 kJ/mol and Δs = 17 J/(mol K). Calculate Tm0 for A, B and C, and state what this implies for designing heat-resistant polymers if the degradation temperature is about 700 K.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: at equilibrium the free energies of crystal and melt are equal, ΔG = Δh − T Δs = 0, so Tm0 = Δh/Δs.\n2. (a) Δh per mole of CH2 = 293 J/g × 14.03 g/mol = 4.11 kJ/mol. Δs = Δh/Tm0 = 4110/414 = 9.9 J/(mol K).\n3. (b) A: Tm0 = 8000/17 = 471 K. B: Tm0 = 8000/8.0 = 1000 K. C: Tm0 = 16 000/17 = 941 K.\n4. Interpretation: B shows that making the chain rigid (small conformational entropy gain on melting) doubles Tm0 without changing the interactions; C shows that stronger interactions (hydrogen bonds) raise Tm0 in proportion to Δh.\n5. With degradation near 700 K, both B and C are predicted to decompose before melting; such polymers cannot be melt processed. Hence the design strategy: maximise Δh (aromatic delocalisation, dipoles, hydrogen bonds) and minimise Δs (rigid or extended chains), but then lower Tm by copolymerisation or add flexible side groups, or process from solution (lyotropic).","answers":["Δs(PE) ≈ 9.9 J/(mol K)","Tm0: A ≈ 471 K, B ≈ 1000 K, C ≈ 941 K","B and C would degrade before melting; use solution processing or kinked comonomers"],"src":"course-style (lecture 10/5)"},{"id":"5.7","title":"Spinning strategies for Kevlar and Vectra fibres","q":"Poly(p-phenylene terephthalamide) (Kevlar) is spun from a nematic solution in concentrated sulphuric acid, whereas the copolyester Vectra (p-hydroxybenzoic acid / 2,6-hydroxynaphthoic acid) is melt processed. (a) Why can Kevlar not be melt spun? (b) How are rigid rod concentration, nematic order and fibre properties linked in the dope? (c) What molecular features make Vectra processable from the melt, and what is the price in properties? (d) Why can UHMWPE fibres reach about 200 GPa even though HDPE has only about 2.5 GPa?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) Kevlar chains are extended and rigid, hydrogen-bonded through the amide groups and densely packed; the melting temperature (Tm0 = Δh/Δs with large Δh and small Δs) is above the decomposition temperature, so it degrades before it melts: only a lyotropic (solution) route is possible.\n2. (b) In concentrated H2SO4 the rods interact and above a critical concentration (Flory: v2* about 8/x) they align spontaneously into a nematic liquid crystal. In the spinneret flow aligns the domains, and the extended, highly oriented chains are locked in on coagulation, giving high strength and modulus. Drawback: large solvent volume per mass of fibre and the handling of acid.\n3. (c) Vectra is an ester (no amide H-bonds) and a statistical copolymer containing kinked naphthalene units; these break the regularity, reduce crystallinity and crystal size and lower Tm into the processable range while a nematic melt with low viscosity is preserved. The price is a lower ultimate chain stiffness/strength and thermal stability than the wholly para-aramid, but production is cheaper.\n4. (d) HDPE is isotropic with randomly oriented, folded chains in which deformation involves weak dispersion bonds; UHMWPE fibre (gel spun and drawn) has almost fully extended, aligned chains so that tension is carried by the covalent C-C backbone, giving about 200 GPa.","answers":["Kevlar degrades before it melts: lyotropic spinning","Nematic dope orients rods; flow and coagulation lock in the alignment","Vectra: ester linkages and naphthalene kinks lower Tm; slightly lower stiffness","UHMWPE: aligned extended chains load covalent bonds"],"src":"course-style (lecture 10/5)"},{"id":"5.8","title":"Emission colour and injection barriers in a polymer light-emitting diode","q":"A PPV-type emissive polymer has its HOMO at 5.1 eV and LUMO at 2.7 eV below the vacuum level (band gap 2.4 eV). The anode is ITO (work function 4.8 eV) and the cathode is calcium (work function 2.9 eV). (a) Calculate the emitted wavelength if the photon energy equals the band gap (hc = 1240 eV nm). (b) Find the hole and electron injection barriers. (c) Explain why a calcium cathode is used and why electron and hole transport layers are added. (d) What fraction of excitons can emit light in a fluorescent polymer and why?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: photon energy from the gap, barrier heights from the offset between electrode Fermi level and the nearest transport level.\n2. (a) λ = hc/Eg = 1240 eV nm/2.4 eV = 517 nm, green light.\n3. (b) Hole barrier from ITO to HOMO: 5.1 − 4.8 = 0.3 eV. Electron barrier from Ca to LUMO: 2.9 − 2.7 = 0.2 eV.\n4. (c) Electrons must be injected at the LUMO (near 2.7 eV), so a low work function metal (high Fermi level, Ca) is needed, while the anode needs a high work function (ITO) to match the deep HOMO. Electrons move more slowly than holes in the disordered polymer, so recombination occurs close to the cathode where the exciton is quenched (heat, non-radiative loss). Hole transport (hTL) and electron transport (eTL) layers balance the carrier transport and move the recombination zone to the middle of the emissive layer.\n5. (d) Recombination produces 25 % singlet and 75 % triplet excitons by spin statistics; triplets do not emit in a fluorescent polymer, so only about 25 % of excitons contribute to the light.","answers":["λ ≈ 517 nm (green)","Hole barrier 0.3 eV; electron barrier 0.2 eV","Low work function Ca for electron injection; transport layers balance carriers and avoid cathode quenching","About 25 % (singlets)"],"src":"course-style (lecture 10/5)"},{"id":"6.1","title":"Pressure-expansion behavior of a rubber balloon","q":"A thin-walled spherical rubber balloon of initial diameter D0 and wall thickness t0 is inflated to diameter D = αD0. Treat the rubber as an incompressible Gaussian (neo-Hookean) network of shear modulus G and assume equal biaxial stretch in the wall. (a) Derive the internal pressure p as a function of α. (b) Find the expansion ratio at which p is maximum. (c) For D0 = 100 mm, t0 = 0.5 mm and G = 1 MPa, find the wall thickness, wall stress and pressure at D = 500 mm, and the maximum pressure.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: wall in plane stress, stretches λ1 = λ2 = α, constant volume gives λ3 = α⁻² (thickness t = t0/α²).\n2. Gaussian network: σ1 - σ3 = G(λ1² - λ3²) with σ3 = 0 at the free surface, so hoop stress σ = G(α² - α⁻⁴).\n3. Force balance on a hemisphere: σ = pD/(4t) = p α³ D0/(4 t0).\n4. (a) p = (4 G t0/D0)(α² - α⁻⁴)/α³ = (4 G t0/D0)(1/α - 1/α⁷).\n5. (b) dp/dα = 0: -1/α² + 7/α⁸ = 0, so α⁶ = 7 and α = 1.38 (a 38 % increase in diameter).\n6. (c) α = 5: t = 0.5/25 = 0.020 mm; σ = 1 MPa × (25 - 0.0016) = 25 MPa; p = 4σt/D = 4 × 25×10⁶ × 20×10⁻⁶ / 0.5 = 4.0×10³ Pa. Also 4Gt0/D0 = 20 kPa, so p(α = 5) = 20 × (0.2 - 1.3×10⁻⁵) = 4.0 kPa.\n7. Maximum: p = 20 kPa × (0.723 - 0.103) = 12.4 kPa at α = 1.38. Beyond the peak less pressure is needed, so inflation of part of the balloon is unstable (the nose of a balloon inflates first), until strain stiffening occurs.","answers":["p = (4G t0/D0)(1/α - 1/α⁷)","Maximum pressure at α = 7^(1/6) = 1.38","At D = 500 mm: t = 20 μm, σ = 25 MPa, p ≈ 4 kPa","p_max ≈ 12.4 kPa"],"src":"Gedde Exercises 3.1-3.3 / McCrum Problem 3.14"},{"id":"6.2","title":"Crosslink density of natural rubber from its modulus","q":"A sample of crosslinked natural rubber (polyisoprene, repeat unit C5H8 with relative atomic masses C = 12 and H = 1) has a shear modulus G = 217 kPa at 20 °C and a density of 909 kg/m³. Assume the mean-squared end-to-end distance of the network strands equals that of the free chains. Find the number of strands (sub-chains) per m³ and the number-average degree of polymerization between crosslinks. Use k = 1.38×10⁻²³ J/K and N_A = 6.023×10²³ mol⁻¹.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: affine theory G = n_s kT (with ⟨r²⟩ ratio 1); strand mass from density.\n2. T = 293 K, so kT = 1.38×10⁻²³ × 293 = 4.04×10⁻²¹ J.\n3. n_s = G/kT = 217×10³ / 4.04×10⁻²¹ = 5.37×10²⁵ strands per m³.\n4. Mass of one strand: ρ/n_s = 909 / 5.37×10²⁵ = 1.69×10⁻²³ kg; per mole: × 6.023×10²³ = 10.2 kg/mol (Mc = 10 200 g/mol).\n5. Repeat unit C5H8 = 5 × 12 + 8 × 1 = 68 g/mol.\n6. Degree of polymerization between crosslinks = 10 200/68 ≈ 150 isoprene units.\nCheck: Mc = ρRT/G = 909 × 8.314 × 293/217×10³ = 10.2 kg/mol, the same result.","answers":["n_s ≈ 5.4×10²⁵ strands per m³","Mc ≈ 10 200 g/mol","≈ 150 isoprene units between crosslinks"],"src":"McCrum Problem 3.7"},{"id":"6.3","title":"Temperature rise on rapid stretching of rubber","q":"An ideal Gaussian rubber with shear modulus G = 500 kPa, density 909 kg/m³ and specific heat capacity 1.92 kJ/(kg K) is stretched rapidly (adiabatically) from the stress-free state to 200 % extension (λ = 3). The internal energy is unchanged on deformation. Estimate the temperature rise, and explain why real rubbers usually warm more at large extension.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: for an ideal rubber dU = 0, so the work done on the rubber is released as heat, and with no heat loss ρ c ΔT = W per unit volume.\n2. Work per unit volume (nominal stress σn = G(λ - λ⁻²)): W = ∫ σn dλ from 1 to λ = G(λ²/2 + 1/λ - 3/2).\n3. For λ = 3: W = 500×10³ × (4.5 + 0.3333 - 1.5) = 500×10³ × 3.333 = 1.67×10⁶ J/m³.\n4. ρ c = 909 × 1920 = 1.745×10⁶ J/(m³ K).\n5. ΔT = 1.67×10⁶ / 1.745×10⁶ = 0.95 K.\n6. Real rubbers warm more at large λ because the stress rises above the Gaussian value (non-Gaussian chains, finite extensibility) so more work is done, and strain-induced crystallization (in NR) releases latent heat; the energetic contribution to the force also adds heat.","answers":["ΔT ≈ 0.95 K","The temperature rises because the work is entirely converted to heat when dU = 0","Extra heating: stress upturn (non-Gaussian) and crystallization"],"src":"McCrum Problem 3.5"},{"id":"6.4","title":"Modulus of a rubber swollen in solvent","q":"A crosslinked rubber of shear modulus 217 kPa in the dry state absorbs liquid until it has swollen to twice its dry volume. Using the affine network picture in which the strands are diluted by the solvent and stretched isotropically, derive the ratio of the swollen to the unswollen modulus in terms of the polymer volume fraction φ, and find the modulus of the swollen rubber.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: G = n_s kT ⟨r²⟩_i/⟨r²⟩_0 per unit volume of the sample.\n2. After swelling the polymer volume fraction is φ; the number of strands per unit swollen volume is diluted to n_s φ.\n3. Isotropic swelling stretches each dimension by λ = φ^(-1/3), so ⟨r²⟩_i/⟨r²⟩_0 = λ² = φ^(-2/3).\n4. G_s = n_s φ kT φ^(-2/3) = φ^(1/3) n_s kT, so G_s/G_u = φ^(1/3) = S^(-1/3) with S = 1/φ the swelling ratio.\n5. Equal volume of liquid absorbed: φ = 0.5, so G_s/G_u = 0.5^(1/3) = 0.794.\n6. G_s = 0.794 × 217 kPa = 172 kPa.\nThe modulus falls although the strands are stretched, since dilution of load-bearing strands dominates.","answers":["G_s/G_u = φ^(1/3) = S^(-1/3)","G_s ≈ 172 kPa (0.17 MPa)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 3.9; course-style (lecture 10/21 gels)"},{"id":"6.5","title":"Mooney-Rivlin stress compared with Gaussian theory","q":"For a certain rubber, uniaxial extension up to 100 % is described by the Mooney-Rivlin strain-energy function with C1 = 300 kPa and C2 = 100 kPa. Find the nominal tensile stress (force per original cross-section) needed for 100 % extension (λ = 2). Then estimate the stress that the Gaussian theory predicts when the shear modulus is taken as G = 2(C1 + C2), and give the percentage error.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Mooney-Rivlin uniaxial nominal stress σn = 2(C1 + C2/λ)(λ - 1/λ²); Gaussian (neo-Hookean) σn = G(λ - 1/λ²).\n2. λ = 2: λ - 1/λ² = 2 - 0.25 = 1.75.\n3. Mooney-Rivlin: C1 + C2/λ = 300 + 50 = 350 kPa, so σn = 2 × 350 × 1.75 = 1225 kPa = 1.23 MPa.\n4. Gaussian: G = 2(C1 + C2) = 800 kPa, so σn = 800 × 1.75 = 1400 kPa = 1.40 MPa.\n5. Error = (1.400 - 1.225)/1.225 = 0.143, i.e. the Gaussian theory overestimates the stress by 14 %.\n6. The Mooney-Rivlin C2 term makes the effective modulus fall with 1/λ: at λ = 2 it is 2(C1 + C2/2) = 700 kPa, below the small-strain 800 kPa.","answers":["Mooney-Rivlin: σn ≈ 1.23 MPa","Gaussian: σn ≈ 1.40 MPa","Gaussian error ≈ 14 % too high"],"src":"McCrum Problem 3.18"},{"id":"6.6","title":"Compression of a rubber cube in one and two directions","q":"A cube of ideal Gaussian rubber of side 100 mm has a number-average molar mass between crosslinks of 5000 g/mol and a density of 900 kg/m³, and is tested at 300 K. Take the strand ratio ⟨r²⟩_i/⟨r²⟩_0 = 1 and R = 8.314 J/(mol K). (a) Find the shear modulus and tensile modulus. (b) A force Fx compresses the cube in the x direction to 75 mm with the y and z faces free; find Fx and the new y and z dimensions. (c) Then compression in the y direction to 75 mm with x held at 75 mm and z free: find the z dimension, Fx = Fy, and the stored strain energy.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. (a) G = ρRT/Mc = 900 × 8.314 × 300 / 5.0 = 4.49×10⁵ Pa = 0.449 MPa; E = 3G = 1.35 MPa.\n2. (b) λx = 0.75; constant volume gives λy = λz = 0.75^(-1/2) = 1.155, so y = z = 115.5 mm.\n3. Stress difference (true stress) σx = G(λx² - λy²) = 0.449×10⁶ × (0.5625 - 1.3333) = -0.346 MPa; area normal to x: 0.1155² = 0.01333 m²; Fx = -4.61 kN.\n4. (c) λx = λy = 0.75 gives λz = 1/(0.75 × 0.75) = 1.778, so z = 177.8 mm.\n5. With σz = 0: σx = σy = G(λx² - λz²) = 0.449×10⁶ × (0.5625 - 3.1605) = -1.166 MPa; area normal to x: 0.075 × 0.1778 = 0.01333 m²; Fx = Fy = -15.5 kN.\n6. Strain energy W = (G/2) V0 (λx² + λy² + λz² - 3) = 0.2245×10⁶ × 10⁻³ × (0.5625 + 0.5625 + 3.1605 - 3) = 224.5 × 1.2855 = 289 J.","answers":["G ≈ 0.449 MPa, E ≈ 1.35 MPa","(b) y = z ≈ 115.5 mm, Fx ≈ -4.6 kN (compressive)","(c) z ≈ 178 mm, Fx = Fy ≈ -15.5 kN, W ≈ 289 J"],"src":"McCrum Problem 3.13"},{"id":"6.7","title":"Choosing an elastomer for a fuel and oil seal","q":"An automotive engineer needs a recyclable seal that stays rubbery from -30 to +100 °C and does not swell badly in hydrocarbon fuel and oil. Candidate materials: (i) uncrosslinked natural rubber, (ii) sulfur-vulcanized natural rubber, (iii) polybutadiene-co-acrylonitrile (nitrile rubber, NBR), (iv) a polystyrene-polybutadiene-polystyrene triblock thermoplastic elastomer. Discuss each against the requirements, say how the crosslink density and the polymer-solvent interaction parameter control swelling according to the Flory-Rehner equation, and recommend a choice.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Requirements: rubbery above Tg for the whole temperature range, elastic (no creep), low swelling in nonpolar fuel, recyclable.\n2. (i) Uncrosslinked NR: rubbery only through entanglements, large creep, dissolves in hydrocarbon fuel: unsuitable.\n3. (ii) Vulcanized NR (S2 to S8 bridges): elastic, no creep, but nonpolar like fuel and oil (χ small), so it swells strongly; not recyclable; sulfur network degrades by air and UV unless stabilized.\n4. (iii) NBR: crosslinked and the polar nitrile group (dipole) raises χ with hydrocarbons, strongly lowering swelling; Tg rises with acrylonitrile content (Fox equation) so composition is a trade-off with low-temperature flexibility.\n5. (iv) SBS: physically crosslinked by glassy PS domains, melt-processable and recyclable, but the PS domains soften at about 100 °C (Tg of PS), and PB is nonpolar so it swells and loses strength in fuel.\n6. Flory-Rehner: ln(1 - v2) + v2 + χ v2² + (ρV1/Mc) v2^(1/3) = 0. Swelling decreases with higher crosslink density (smaller Mc) and with a larger χ (poor solvent for the rubber); it is limited by the entropic force of the stretched strands.\n7. Recommendation: NBR with moderate acrylonitrile content, accepting that it is not recyclable (permanent crosslinks).","answers":["NBR (crosslinked, polar nitrile suppresses swelling in fuel and oil)","Uncrosslinked NR dissolves, vulcanized NR swells, SBS swells and softens near 100 °C","Swelling falls with smaller Mc and larger χ (Flory-Rehner)"],"src":"course-style (lecture 10/21)"},{"id":"6.8","title":"Energetic share of the rubber force from stress-temperature data","q":"A rubber strip is held at constant elongation λ = 2 (unstressed length measured at each temperature) at constant pressure. The force measured is 1.200 N at 300 K and 1.272 N at 320 K. (a) Use f = (∂E/∂L) + T(∂f/∂T) to find the entropic and energetic parts of the force at 300 K and the ratio fe/f, taking the slope from the two points. (b) Compare with the prediction for an ideal entropy-elastic rubber. (c) Explain why, when the sample is held at constant length L (with L0 fixed at the starting temperature), the stress of natural rubber at λ \u003c 1.1 decreases with temperature while at larger λ it increases.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the entropic force is T(∂f/∂T)_{p,λ}; the energetic part fe = f - T(∂f/∂T).\n2. Slope: (∂f/∂T) = (1.272 - 1.200)/(320 - 300) = 0.0036 N/K.\n3. Entropic part at 300 K: T(∂f/∂T) = 300 × 0.0036 = 1.08 N, which is 90 % of the force.\n4. Energetic part: fe = 1.200 - 1.08 = 0.12 N, so fe/f = 0.10 (consistent with the 0.1-0.2 typical for natural rubber, e.g. +0.17).\n5. (b) An ideal entropy-elastic rubber has fe = 0 and f ∝ T: f(320 K) = 1.200 × 320/300 = 1.280 N, close to the measured 1.272 N, confirming mostly entropic behavior.\n6. (c) At constant length L, heating expands the sample (L0 increases), so the true λ = L/L0 falls; at small λ this thermal expansion dominates and the force decreases (thermoelastic inversion near λ ≈ 1.1). At larger λ the entropic term T(∂f/∂T) grows with λ and dominates, so the stress rises with T. If L0 is taken at each temperature, the coefficient is positive at all λ.","answers":["Entropic force ≈ 1.08 N (90 %), energetic force ≈ 0.12 N, fe/f ≈ 0.10","Ideal rubber would give 1.28 N at 320 K (measured 1.272 N)","Inversion at λ ≈ 1.1 is due to thermal expansion at fixed length"],"src":"Gedde §3.2 / McCrum Problems 3.3-3.4 (course-style numbers)"},{"id":"7.1","title":"Creep of a polymer rod under a hanging mass","q":"A straight rod of linearly viscoelastic polymer, length 1 m and diameter 10 mm, has tensile creep compliance D(t) = 2 − exp(−0.1 t) GPa⁻¹, with t in hours. A mass of 10 kg is hung from the rod for 10 hours (g = 9.81 m/s²). Find the change in length of the rod at the end of the 10 hours.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: constant stress for 10 h, so creep strain = σ0·D(10 h) (linear viscoelasticity, eq. ε(t)=σ0 D(t)).\n2. Stress: area A = π(0.005 m)² = 7.854×10⁻⁵ m²; force F = 10 × 9.81 = 98.1 N; σ0 = F/A = 1.249 MPa.\n3. Compliance at t = 10 h: D = 2 − exp(−1) = 2 − 0.3679 = 1.632 GPa⁻¹ = 1.632×10⁻⁹ Pa⁻¹.\n4. Strain: ε = σ0 D = 1.249×10⁶ × 1.632×10⁻⁹ = 2.04×10⁻³ (0.2 %, inside the linear range).\n5. Length change: ΔL = ε L = 2.04×10⁻³ × 1 m = 2.04 mm.","answers":["Creep strain 2.04×10⁻³","Elongation ΔL ≈ 2.04 mm"],"src":"McCrum Problem 4.1"},{"id":"7.2","title":"Clamping force loss in a tightened nylon bolt","q":"A nylon bolt of diameter 8 mm joins two rigid plates. The nylon is linearly viscoelastic with tensile relaxation modulus E(t) = 5 exp(−t^(1/3)) GPa, t in hours. The bolt is tightened quickly so that its initial force is 1 kN, after which its length is held constant. (1) Find the strain in the bolt. (2) Find the force remaining after 24 hours.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: constant strain (stress relaxation), so σ(t) = ε0 E(t).\n2. Initial stress: A = π(4 mm)² = 50.27 mm² = 5.027×10⁻⁵ m²; σ(0) = 1000 N / A = 19.89 MPa.\n3. Strain: E(0) = 5 GPa, so ε0 = σ(0)/E(0) = 19.89×10⁶ / 5×10⁹ = 3.98×10⁻³. This strain stays constant.\n4. Modulus at 24 h: t^(1/3) = 24^(1/3) = 2.884; E(24 h) = 5 exp(−2.884) = 5 × 0.0559 = 0.279 GPa.\n5. Stress after 24 h: σ = ε0 E = 3.98×10⁻³ × 0.279×10⁹ = 1.11 MPa.\n6. Force: F = σ A = 1.11×10⁶ × 5.027×10⁻⁵ ≈ 56 N, i.e. only about 5.6 % of the initial clamping force remains.","answers":["Strain ε0 = 3.98×10⁻³ (0.4 %)","Force after 24 h ≈ 56 N (about 5.6 % of 1 kN)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 4.2"},{"id":"7.3","title":"Creep and complex compliance of a two-element model","q":"A polymer in shear is represented by an instantaneous spring J_U in series with two parallel spring-dashpot (Kelvin) elements, with J_U = J₀, J₁ = 2J₀, τ₁ = τ₀ and J₂ = 3J₀, τ₂ = 2τ₀. Find (a) the creep compliance after a constant stress has acted for t = 3τ₀, and (b) the complex compliance J* = J′ − iJ″ at angular frequency ω = 1/(3τ₀).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Model: generalized Zener (Kelvin chain). Creep: J(t) = J_U + Σ Jᵢ[1 − exp(−t/τᵢ)]. Dynamic: J*(ω) = J_U + Σ Jᵢ/(1 + iωτᵢ).\n2. (a) At t = 3τ₀: J = J₀ + 2J₀(1 − e⁻³) + 3J₀(1 − e^(−1.5)) = J₀[1 + 2(0.9502) + 3(0.7769)] = J₀(1 + 1.9004 + 2.3306) = 5.23 J₀.\n3. (b) ωτ₁ = 1/3, ωτ₂ = 2/3.\n   Element 1: 2J₀/(1 + i/3) = 2J₀(1 − i/3)/(1 + 1/9) = J₀(1.800 − 0.600 i).\n   Element 2: 3J₀/(1 + 2i/3) = 3J₀(1 − 2i/3)/(1 + 4/9) = J₀(2.077 − 1.385 i).\n4. Sum with J_U: J′ = J₀(1 + 1.800 + 2.077) = 4.88 J₀; J″ = J₀(0.600 + 1.385) = 1.98 J₀.\n5. Loss tangent: tan δ = J″/J′ = 0.41 (δ ≈ 22°).","answers":["(a) J(3τ₀) = 5.23 J₀","(b) J* = (4.88 − 1.98 i) J₀, i.e. J′ = 4.88 J₀, J″ = 1.98 J₀, tan δ ≈ 0.41"],"src":"McCrum Problem 4.14"},{"id":"7.4","title":"Power dissipated in an oscillated polymer bar","q":"A bar of polymer, length 60 mm with a 10 mm × 4 mm rectangular cross-section, carries a force along its length that oscillates sinusoidally between −150 N and +150 N at 50 Hz. At 50 Hz the imaginary part of its complex tensile compliance is D″ = 3.7×10⁻⁹ Pa⁻¹. Find the power input needed to sustain the oscillation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: energy dissipated per unit volume per cycle for stress amplitude σ0 is ΔW = π D″ σ0² (only the out-of-phase compliance contributes; the D′ part integrates to zero over a cycle).\n2. Stress amplitude: A = 10 mm × 4 mm = 40 mm² = 4.0×10⁻⁵ m²; σ0 = 150 / 4.0×10⁻⁵ = 3.75 MPa.\n3. Per unit volume per cycle: ΔW = π × 3.7×10⁻⁹ × (3.75×10⁶)² = π × 3.7×10⁻⁹ × 1.406×10¹³ = 1.63×10⁵ J m⁻³.\n4. Volume: V = 40 mm² × 60 mm = 2.4×10⁻⁶ m³, so energy per cycle = 1.63×10⁵ × 2.4×10⁻⁶ = 0.392 J.\n5. Power: P = 0.392 J × 50 s⁻¹ ≈ 19.6 W.\n6. Comment: this appears as heat in the bar, so the specimen temperature rises unless heat is lost.","answers":["Dissipated energy ≈ 0.39 J per cycle","Power input ≈ 19.6 W (all converted to heat)"],"src":"McCrum Example 4.3"},{"id":"7.5","title":"Torsion of a PVC pipe with a temperature history","q":"A PVC pipe of length 2 m, outside diameter 40 mm and wall thickness 2 mm carries a torque of 8 N·m for 20 hours. At 20 °C its shear creep compliance is J(t) = 0.75 + 0.15 log₁₀t + 0.018 (log₁₀t)² GPa⁻¹, t in seconds. Time-temperature superposition holds and the shift factor at 60 °C relative to 20 °C is a_T = 10⁻³. Treat the pipe as a thin-walled tube of radius equal to the outside radius (20 mm). Find the relative rotation of the pipe ends if the temperature is (1) 20 °C throughout, (2) 60 °C throughout, (3) 20 °C for 19.75 h followed by 60 °C for 0.25 h.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Thin tube: shear stress τ = T/(2π r² s) = 8/(2π × 0.02² × 0.002) = 1.592 MPa; strain γ = τ J; rotation θ = γ L/r (L = 2 m, r = 0.02 m, so θ = 100 γ).\n2. (1) t = 20 h = 72 000 s, log t = 4.857: J = 0.75 + 0.7286 + 0.4247 = 1.903 GPa⁻¹; γ = 1.592×10⁶ × 1.903×10⁻⁹ = 3.03×10⁻³; θ = 0.303 rad = 17.4°.\n3. (2) At 60 °C, J⁶⁰(t) = J²⁰(t/a_T) = J²⁰(1000 t): effective log t = 4.857 + 3 = 7.857; J = 0.75 + 1.1786 + 1.1113 = 3.040 GPa⁻¹; γ = 4.84×10⁻³; θ = 0.484 rad = 27.7°.\n4. (3) Under constant stress with changing temperature use reduced time ξ = ∫dt/a_T: 19.75 h at a_T = 1 plus 0.25 h at a_T = 10⁻³ gives ξ = 19.75 + 250 = 269.75 h = 9.71×10⁵ s, log ξ = 5.987.\n5. J = 0.75 + 0.8981 + 0.6453 = 2.293 GPa⁻¹; γ = 3.65×10⁻³; θ = 0.365 rad = 20.9°.\n6. Comment: 15 min at 60 °C adds as much creep as about 250 h at 20 °C.","answers":["(1) θ ≈ 0.303 rad = 17.4°","(2) θ ≈ 0.484 rad = 27.7°","(3) θ ≈ 0.365 rad = 20.9°"],"src":"McCrum Problem 4.16"},{"id":"7.6","title":"Thermal stress in a constrained polypropylene pipe","q":"A polypropylene pipe fixed rigidly at both ends is stress-free at 20 °C. Its tensile relaxation modulus at 20 °C is E(t) = 2 t^(−0.09) GPa (t in s) and its thermal expansion coefficient is 10⁻⁴ K⁻¹. Hot water suddenly raises it to 50 °C for one hour, after which it suddenly returns to 20 °C. The pipe obeys Boltzmann superposition and time-temperature superposition with an Arrhenius shift factor of activation enthalpy 145 kJ/mol. Find the stress in the pipe 100 s after the return to 20 °C (R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: ends fixed, so the mechanical strain is minus the thermal strain; apply Boltzmann superposition to the two strain steps.\n2. Steps: at t = 0 the mechanical strain jumps by −αΔT = −10⁻⁴ × 30 = −3×10⁻³ (compression); at t = 3600 s it jumps back by +3×10⁻³.\n3. Shift factor of 50 °C relative to 20 °C: a_T = exp[(145 000/8.314)(1/323.15 − 1/293.15)] = exp(−5.523) = 3.99×10⁻³. Relaxation at 50 °C corresponds to 20 °C relaxation at reduced time t/a_T.\n4. Reduced time elapsed since the first step when the second step has aged 100 s: 3600/3.99×10⁻³ + 100 = 9.03×10⁵ s.\n5. Moduli: E(100 s) = 2 × 100^(−0.09) = 1.321 GPa; E(9.03×10⁵ s) = 2 × (9.03×10⁵)^(−0.09) = 0.582 GPa.\n6. Superposition: σ = (−3×10⁻³)(0.582 GPa) + (+3×10⁻³)(1.321 GPa) = 3×10⁻³ × 0.739 GPa = +2.22 MPa.\n7. Meaning: the compressive stress relaxed during the hot hour, so the cool-down leaves a net tensile stress.","answers":["Shift factor a_T(50 °C) = 3.99×10⁻³","Stress ≈ +2.22 MPa (tensile) 100 s after the return to 20 °C"],"src":"McCrum Problem 4.17"},{"id":"7.7","title":"Predicting long-term creep from short hot tests","q":"(a) A semicrystalline polymer has a creep relaxation process with activation enthalpy 100 kJ/mol obeying Arrhenius shifting. How long must a creep test at 60 °C last to represent 5 years of loading at 25 °C? (b) For atactic polystyrene (T_g = 100 °C) the modulus at 110 °C reaches a given value after 10 s. Using the universal WLF constants, how long does it take to reach the same modulus at 100 °C? Use R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: equivalent time at the reference temperature is t/a_T; use Arrhenius for (a) and WLF for (b).\n2. (a) a_T(60 °C relative to 25 °C) = exp[(ΔH/R)(1/333.15 − 1/298.15)] = exp[12 028 × (−3.524×10⁻⁴)] = exp(−4.238) = 1.44×10⁻².\n3. 5 years = 5 × 8766 h = 43 830 h at 25 °C, so the test time at 60 °C is t_test = a_T × 43 830 h = 1.44×10⁻² × 43 830 ≈ 630 h, about 26 days.\n4. (b) WLF with T_r = T_g: log a_T = −17.44 (T − T_g)/(51.6 + T − T_g); at 110 °C, T − T_g = 10 K: log a_T = −174.4/61.6 = −2.831, a_T = 1.48×10⁻³ (110 °C relative to T_g).\n5. A process taking 10 s at 110 °C takes 10 s / a_T = 10 / 1.48×10⁻³ = 6.8×10³ s ≈ 1.9 h at 100 °C.\n6. Caveats: ageing, oxidation, further crystallization or moisture change can invalidate long extrapolations; WLF is valid only between T_g and T_g + 100 K.","answers":["(a) a_T ≈ 1.44×10⁻²; test at 60 °C for about 630 h (≈ 26 days)","(b) a_T ≈ 1.48×10⁻³; about 6.8×10³ s (≈ 1.9 h) at T_g"],"src":"course-style (McCrum §4.3.3, Gedde §5.4)"},{"id":"7.8","title":"Zener solid: characteristic times and loss maxima","q":"A polymer is modelled as a Zener solid (standard linear solid) with unrelaxed shear modulus G_U = 2 GPa, relaxed modulus G_R = 0.2 GPa and relaxation time at constant strain τ_ε = 5 s. Find (a) the creep (retardation) time τ_σ, (b) J(t) at t = τ_σ, (c) G′ and G″ at ω = 1/τ_ε, and (d) the maximum value of tan δ and the angular frequency where it occurs. (e) Why would a real polymer need more than one element?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Relations: τ_ε/τ_σ = G_R/G_U = J_U/J_R; J_U = 1/G_U, J_R = 1/G_R.\n2. (a) τ_σ = τ_ε G_U/G_R = 5 × 10 = 50 s.\n3. (b) J_U = 0.5 GPa⁻¹, J_R = 5 GPa⁻¹. J(τ_σ) = J_R − (J_R − J_U) e⁻¹ = 5 − 4.5 × 0.3679 = 3.34 GPa⁻¹.\n4. (c) With x = ωτ_ε = 1 and ΔG = G_U − G_R = 1.8 GPa: G′ = G_R + ΔG x²/(1+x²) = 0.2 + 0.9 = 1.1 GPa; G″ = ΔG x/(1+x²) = 0.9 GPa (the maximum of G″).\n5. (d) tan δ = ΔG x/(G_R + G_U x²), maximal at x² = G_R/G_U, i.e. ω = (τ_σ τ_ε)^(−1/2) = 1/√250 = 0.063 rad/s. tan δ_max = (G_U − G_R)/(2√(G_U G_R)) = 1.8/(2 × 0.632) = 1.42.\n6. (e) The single exponential completes in about 3 decades, whereas real relaxations (heterogeneous environments, chain-length distribution) are broader, so a spectrum of relaxation times (many Maxwell or Kelvin elements) is needed.","answers":["(a) τ_σ = 50 s","(b) J(τ_σ) = 3.34 GPa⁻¹","(c) G′ = 1.1 GPa, G″ = 0.9 GPa at ω = 0.2 rad/s","(d) tan δ_max ≈ 1.42 at ω ≈ 0.063 rad/s","(e) Real relaxations are broader than a single exponential: use a spectrum of times"],"src":"course-style (McCrum §4.3.1, Figs. 4.15-4.16)"},{"id":"8.1","title":"Capillary rheometer data for polypropylene melt","q":"A capillary rheometer with a die of diameter 2 mm measures the pressure drop ΔP (MPa) of polypropylene at 190 °C for three die lengths L and three volume flow rates Q (10⁻⁷ m³/s). L = 5 mm: 1.502, 2.100, 2.935; L = 10 mm: 2.337, 3.266, 4.565; L = 20 mm: 4.006, 5.599, 7.826 (for Q = 2, 4, 8 respectively). (1) For each Q find the apparent viscosity assuming Newtonian behaviour, eliminating the entry pressure loss. (2) Find the power-law exponent n and, with the Rabinowitsch correction, the true wall shear rate and viscosity for each Q.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the entry (Bagley) loss is independent of L, so the slope ΔP/L from the length series is the pressure gradient in the capillary. Wall stress τ_w = RΔP/(2L), R = 1 mm; apparent wall rate γ̇_a = 4Q/(πR³).\n2. Slopes (L = 5 to 20 mm): Q = 2: 0.1669 MPa/mm; Q = 4: 0.2333; Q = 8: 0.3261 (the three lengths are collinear). Then τ_w = (10⁻³/2) × slope = 8.35×10⁴, 1.166×10⁵, 1.630×10⁵ Pa.\n3. γ̇_a = 4Q/(πR³) = 254.6, 509.3, 1019 s⁻¹. (1) η_a = τ_w/γ̇_a = 328, 229, 160 Pa s.\n4. (2) Slope of log τ_w versus log γ̇_a: n = log(1.630×10⁵/8.35×10⁴)/log 4 = 0.483.\n5. Rabinowitsch: γ̇_w = [(3n+1)/(4n)] γ̇_a = 1.268 γ̇_a = 323, 646, 1291 s⁻¹.\n6. True viscosity η = τ_w/γ̇_w = 259, 181, 126 Pa s (about 20 % lower than the Newtonian estimate).","answers":["(1) η_a = 328, 229, 160 Pa s for Q = 2, 4, 8 ×10⁻⁷ m³/s","(2) n = 0.483; γ̇_w = 323, 646, 1291 s⁻¹; η = 259, 181, 126 Pa s"],"src":"McCrum Problems 7.6-7.7"},{"id":"8.2","title":"Viscosity sensitivity of polystyrene melt to temperature","q":"Polystyrene has T_g = 97 °C. Find the fractional change of melt viscosity per kelvin (1) at 170 °C, assuming the WLF equation with the universal constants C₁ = 17.44 and C₂ = 51.6 K referred to T_g, and (2) at 230 °C, assuming an Arrhenius law with activation energy 104 kJ/mol (R = 8.314 J mol⁻¹ K⁻¹). (3) By roughly how many kelvin must the melt be heated to halve its viscosity at each temperature?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: differentiate ln η with respect to T. For WLF, log₁₀ a_T = −C₁(T−T_g)/(C₂+T−T_g), so d ln η/dT = −2.303 C₁C₂/(C₂+T−T_g)². For Arrhenius, d ln η/dT = −E_a/(RT²).\n2. (1) T − T_g = 73 K; C₂ + T − T_g = 124.6 K. d ln η/dT = −2.303 × 17.44 × 51.6/124.6² = −2072/15 525 = −0.1335 K⁻¹, i.e. −13.3 % per kelvin.\n3. (2) T = 503.15 K: RT² = 8.314 × 2.5316×10⁵ = 2.105×10⁶ J/mol; d ln η/dT = −104 000/2.105×10⁶ = −0.0494 K⁻¹, i.e. −4.9 % per kelvin (book: −4.95).\n4. (3) Halving needs ΔT ≈ ln 2/|d ln η/dT| = 0.693/0.1335 ≈ 5 K at 170 °C and 0.693/0.0494 ≈ 14 K at 230 °C.\n5. Comment: near T_g (WLF region) viscosity is far more temperature sensitive than far above T_g (Arrhenius), so temperature control matters most for low-temperature processing.","answers":["(1) −13.3 % per K at 170 °C (WLF)","(2) −4.9 % per K at 230 °C (Arrhenius)","(3) About 5 K at 170 °C and 14 K at 230 °C to halve the viscosity"],"src":"McCrum Problem 7.8"},{"id":"8.3","title":"Power-law melt flow through an annular tube die","q":"A tube is extruded through an annular die of mean diameter 50 mm and gap 2 mm. The melt obeys the power law and has apparent viscosities of 4500 Pa s at a shear rate of 10 s⁻¹ and 1000 Pa s at 100 s⁻¹. A pressure drop per unit length of 10⁸ Pa/m is applied along the die. Treat the thin annulus as a flat slit of width πD. Find the power-law exponent n, the consistency K, the wall shear rate and the volume flow rate.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Power law: η_a = Kγ̇^(n−1). Ratio of viscosities: 1000/4500 = 10^(n−1), so n − 1 = log(0.2222) = −0.653, n = 0.347.\n2. K = 4500/10^(−0.653) = 4500/0.2222 = 2.03×10⁴ Pa sⁿ.\n3. Slit: shear stress is zero on the centreline and τ_w = (ΔP/L)(h/2) = 10⁸ × 0.001 = 1.0×10⁵ Pa at the walls. Wall shear rate γ̇_w = (τ_w/K)^(1/n) = (4.94)^(2.88) ≈ 100 s⁻¹ (consistent: η_a = 1000 Pa s).\n4. Flow rate through a power-law slit: Q = (w h²/2)[n/(2n+1)] (hΔP/(2KL))^(1/n) = (w h²/2)[n/(2n+1)] γ̇_w.\n5. Values: w = πD = 0.1571 m; h² = 4×10⁻⁶ m²; w h²/2 = 3.14×10⁻⁷ m³; n/(2n+1) = 0.347/1.694 = 0.205.\n6. Q = 3.14×10⁻⁷ × 0.205 × 100 = 6.4×10⁻⁶ m³/s (about 6.4 cm³/s).","answers":["n = 0.347; K ≈ 2.03×10⁴ Pa sⁿ","Wall shear rate ≈ 100 s⁻¹","Q ≈ 6.4×10⁻⁶ m³/s"],"src":"McCrum Problem 7.13"},{"id":"8.4","title":"Mould shrinkage of an injection-moulded polystyrene plate","q":"A polystyrene plate is injection moulded. When the gate freezes and seals the cavity, the melt is at 120 °C and 20 MPa. Assume slow, uniform cooling in the sealed cavity, and thereafter free contraction. Data: volume thermal expansion coefficient 6.0×10⁻⁴ K⁻¹ above T_g and 2.0×10⁻⁴ K⁻¹ below it, T_g = 97 °C, compressibility of the melt at 120 °C 0.55 GPa⁻¹. Predict the linear shrinkage of the plate when it is removed from the mould at 20 °C.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: after sealing the specific volume stays constant while pressure falls with temperature, until the pressure reaches 1 atm (neglect 0.1 MPa); then the plate contracts freely. Shrinkage is relative to the mould cavity.\n2. Constant-volume cooling: dP/dT = α/β = 6.0×10⁻⁴ / 0.55×10⁻⁹ = 1.09×10⁶ Pa/K.\n3. Pressure falls to zero after ΔT = 20×10⁶ / 1.09×10⁶ = 18.3 K, at T = 101.7 °C (still above T_g = 97 °C).\n4. Free contraction 101.7 °C to 97 °C (rubbery state): ΔV/V = 6.0×10⁻⁴ × 4.7 = 2.8×10⁻³.\n5. Free contraction 97 °C to 20 °C (glassy): ΔV/V = 2.0×10⁻⁴ × 77 = 1.54×10⁻².\n6. Total volume shrinkage = 1.82×10⁻²; linear shrinkage = (1/3) × 1.82×10⁻² = 6.1×10⁻³ = 0.61 %.\n7. Comment: the packing pressure keeps the part in contact with the mould until 102 °C, so most of the rubbery-state contraction is compensated by extra melt.","answers":["Pressure reaches 1 atm at about 102 °C","Volume shrinkage 1.8 %; linear mould shrinkage ≈ 0.61 %"],"src":"McCrum Problem 7.25"},{"id":"8.5","title":"Packing pressure to limit shrinkage of a polyethylene moulding","q":"A polyethylene melt obeys the equation of state (10⁻³P + 3.2×10⁵)(10³v − 0.822) = 2.9×10² T, with P in Pa, v the specific volume in m³/kg and T in K. In the solid state at 20 °C its density is 970 kg/m³. (1) Find the free linear shrinkage of a block cooled uniformly from 130 °C (at atmospheric pressure) to 20 °C. (2) In injection moulding the cavity is sealed with the melt at 130 °C; assuming slow uniform cooling, find the packing pressure needed so that the linear mould shrinkage on cooling to 20 °C does not exceed 2 %.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Free shrinkage: melt at 130 °C = 403.15 K and P = 1.013×10⁵ Pa: 10⁻³P = 101.3, so (101.3 + 3.2×10⁵)(10³v − 0.822) = 290 × 403.15 = 1.169×10⁵. Thus 10³v − 0.822 = 0.3652 and v_m = 1.187×10⁻³ m³/kg.\n2. Solid: v_s = 1/970 = 1.0309×10⁻³ m³/kg. Volume ratio v_s/v_m = 0.8684; linear ratio = 0.8684^(1/3) = 0.9541, so free linear shrinkage = 4.6 %.\n3. (2) With the gate frozen the specific volume in the cavity is constant until P falls to 1 atm; the final solid volume per unit mass is v_s. Mould-referred shrinkage ≤ 2 %: v_s ≥ v₀ (0.98)³, so v₀ = 1.0309×10⁻³/0.9412 = 1.0953×10⁻³ m³/kg.\n4. At T = 403.15 K the sealed state must satisfy the equation of state with this v₀: 10³v₀ − 0.822 = 0.2734.\n5. Hence 10⁻³P + 3.2×10⁵ = 1.169×10⁵/0.2734 = 4.277×10⁵, so 10⁻³P = 1.077×10⁵ and P ≈ 1.08×10⁸ Pa.","answers":["(1) Free linear shrinkage ≈ 4.6 %","(2) Packing pressure ≈ 108 MPa (about 1070 atm)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 7.26"},{"id":"8.6","title":"Operating point of a single-screw extruder and film die","q":"A single-start metering screw has barrel diameter D = 150 mm, pitch 150 mm, channel depth H = 9 mm, flight width e = 15 mm, axial metering length 1.00 m and speed 100 rev/min. The melt is Newtonian with viscosity 300 Pa s. It feeds a flat film die of width 0.5 m, gap 2 mm and land length 20 mm; neglect leakage and entry/exit losses. (a) Find the open-discharge output of the screw and its pressure-driven back-flow coefficient (flow lost per Pa). (b) Find the operating point (Q, ΔP). (c) Find the new operating point if the die gap is reduced to 1 mm.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Geometry: tan φ = pitch/(πD) = 0.3183, φ = 17.7°; cos φ = 0.953, sin φ = 0.303. Barrel speed N = 100/60 = 1.667 s⁻¹.\n2. V = πDN cos φ = 0.7854 × 0.953 = 0.748 m/s; channel width W = p cos φ − e = 0.1429 − 0.015 = 0.128 m; channel length L_c = 1.00/sin φ = 3.30 m.\n3. (a) Drag flow Q_d = ½ W H V = 0.5 × 0.128 × 0.009 × 0.748 = 4.31×10⁻⁴ m³/s. Back-flow coefficient c_p = W H³/(12 μ L_c) = 0.128 × 7.29×10⁻⁷/(12 × 300 × 3.30) = 7.9×10⁻¹² m³ s⁻¹ Pa⁻¹.\n4. Screw characteristic: Q = Q_d − c_p ΔP.\n5. Die: Q = k_d ΔP, k_d = w h³/(12 μ L_d) = 0.5 × 8×10⁻⁹/(12 × 300 × 0.02) = 5.56×10⁻¹¹ m³ s⁻¹ Pa⁻¹.\n6. (b) Equate: ΔP = Q_d/(c_p + k_d) = 4.31×10⁻⁴/6.34×10⁻¹¹ = 6.8 MPa; Q = k_d ΔP = 3.8×10⁻⁴ m³/s (88 % of open discharge).\n7. (c) h = 1 mm: k_d falls by 8 to 6.9×10⁻¹²; ΔP = 4.31×10⁻⁴/(7.9×10⁻¹² + 6.9×10⁻¹²) = 29 MPa; Q = 2.0×10⁻⁴ m³/s.","answers":["(a) Q_d ≈ 4.3×10⁻⁴ m³/s; c_p ≈ 7.9×10⁻¹² m³ s⁻¹ Pa⁻¹","(b) Q ≈ 3.8×10⁻⁴ m³/s at ΔP ≈ 6.8 MPa","(c) Q ≈ 2.0×10⁻⁴ m³/s at ΔP ≈ 29 MPa"],"src":"course-style (McCrum Example 7.5, Note 7.N.7, Problems 7.15-7.16)"},{"id":"8.7","title":"Cooling time of an extruded polymer plate","q":"A 3 mm thick plate of ABS (thermal diffusivity 1.2×10⁻⁷ m²/s) leaves the die at 190 °C and passes through a water bath at 15 °C. Assume the surface immediately takes the water temperature (very large Biot number) and conduction through the thickness is one-dimensional. To avoid distortion the through-thickness mean temperature must be at most 60 °C when the plate leaves the bath. Find the minimum cooling time, and the time if the plate thickness is doubled. Use the first term of the series solution.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Model: plate of half-thickness L = 1.5 mm cooled at both faces, T = T_e at the surface. Mean temperature (first Fourier term): (T̄ − T_e)/(T_i − T_e) = (8/π²) exp(−π² Fo/4), with Fo = αt/L².\n2. Required ratio: (60 − 15)/(190 − 15) = 0.2571.\n3. exp(−π² Fo/4) = 0.2571 × π²/8 = 0.3172; −π² Fo/4 = ln 0.3172 = −1.148, so Fo = 1.148 × 4/π² = 0.465.\n4. Fo \u003e 0.2, so a single term is accurate.\n5. Time: t = Fo L²/α = 0.465 × (1.5×10⁻³)²/1.2×10⁻⁷ = 0.465 × 18.75 s = 8.7 s.\n6. Doubling the thickness (L = 3 mm): t scales with L², so t = 4 × 8.7 = 35 s.\n7. Comment: cooling time grows as thickness squared, so thick sections (ribs, bosses) set the cycle time and should be thinner than the wall.","answers":["Minimum cooling time ≈ 8.7 s (Fo ≈ 0.47)","Doubling thickness: ≈ 35 s (four times longer)"],"src":"course-style (McCrum §7.3, Problem 7.23)"},{"id":"8.8","title":"Designing a creep-limited nylon bracket for service","q":"A designer wants to replace a steel bracket by an injection-moulded 33 % glass-filled nylon 6.6 part that must carry a constant load for 2 years at 60 °C in a humid factory, with a stiffness (deflection) limit. Outline (a) how to choose the modulus for the stress analysis and where the data come from, (b) two effects besides creep that lower stiffness or strength in this service, and (c) two moulding-related design rules that affect the part.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use the pseudo-elastic design method of McCrum ch. 8 with polymer-specific data.\n2. (a) Fix the maximum temperature (60 °C), the loading time (2 years ≈ 6.3×10⁷ s) and the allowable strain/deflection (about 1 % for glass-filled nylon, a brittle grade). From creep curves of the grade at 60 °C and the relevant humidity, replot isochronous stress-strain curves for 6.3×10⁷ s; read the secant creep modulus E_c = σ/ε at the design strain; use E_c in standard elastic beam formulae (iterate if deflection-limited). Poisson\u0027s ratio taken constant.\n3. (b) Water absorption plasticizes nylon, lowering T_g and the modulus (a drop from about 3 GPa dry to about 0.5 GPa at saturation at room temperature); so use conditioned (wet) data. Physical ageing and moulding stress relaxation, anisotropy from fibre orientation along flow, and weld lines also change properties.\n4. (c) Keep wall thickness uniform and ribs about 60 % of the wall; place gates and weld lines away from highly stressed regions; allow draft angles and anisotropic shrinkage for fibres; consider notch sensitivity (low failure strain).\n5. Metal-style design (constant E, no time or moisture effects) would over-predict stiffness.","answers":["Use the creep (secant) modulus for 60 °C, 2 years, design strain and conditioned (wet) data in elastic formulae","Moisture plasticization (and ageing, fibre orientation or weld lines) cut stiffness and strength","Design rules: uniform walls, thinner ribs, gate and weld lines away from stress, draft and shrinkage allowance"],"src":"course-style (McCrum ch. 8, lecture 11/23)"},{"id":"9.1","title":"Orientation function of three planar molecules","q":"Three planar chain molecules, each drawn with bonds of equal length, lie in the plane of the figure with a common director D pointing up. Molecule (a) is U-shaped: 2 bonds are horizontal (perpendicular to D) and 8 bonds are vertical (parallel to D). Molecule (b) is a straight chain of 6 bonds, all parallel to D. Molecule (c) is a closed square loop of 8 bonds, 4 horizontal and 4 vertical. For a two-dimensional distribution the orientation function is f = 2\u003ccos²φ\u003e − 1. Calculate f for each molecule.","figs":["figures/ex-poly/ex9-1.webp"],"figDesc":"Three planar molecules built of bonds (dots joined by lines) with a common director D (arrow pointing up): (a) a U shape with two vertical legs of four bonds each joined by a horizontal top of two bonds; (b) a straight vertical chain of seven atoms; (c) a closed square loop with three atoms (two bonds) per side.","sol":"1. Approach: average cos²φ over bonds, where φ is the angle between each bond and the director D. In 2-D a bond parallel to D has cos²φ = 1, a bond perpendicular to D has cos²φ = 0.\n2. Relation: \u003ccos²φ\u003e = n_par / n_total, so f = 2 n_par / n_total − 1.\n3. (a) n_par = 8, n_total = 10: \u003ccos²φ\u003e = 0.8, f = 2(0.8) − 1 = 0.6.\n4. (b) all 6 bonds parallel: \u003ccos²φ\u003e = 1, f = 1.\n5. (c) 4 of 8 bonds parallel: \u003ccos²φ\u003e = 0.5, f = 2(0.5) − 1 = 0.\n6. Check: f = 1 for perfect alignment and f = 0 for the 2-D random case (\u003ccos²φ\u003e = 1/2).","answers":["(a) f = 0.6","(b) f = 1","(c) f = 0"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.1"},{"id":"9.2","title":"Why polymers differ in intrinsic birefringence","q":"The maximum (intrinsic) birefringence Δn0 = n_c − n_t, where n_c is the refractive index along the chain axis and n_t the index transverse to it, is: poly(ethylene terephthalate) +0.25, polycarbonate +0.24, poly(vinyl chloride) +0.01, polystyrene −0.16. Discuss the molecular reasons for these large differences, and state what the sign means for the refractive index of a drawn sample measured along the draw direction.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the refractive index tensor follows the polarizability tensor (Lorentz-Lorenz); Δn0 is proportional to p1 − p2, the difference between the segment polarizability along (p1) and perpendicular to (p2) the chain axis.\n2. PET and PC: strongly polarizable phenylene rings lie in the main chain with their long polarizable axis along the chain, so p1 \u003e p2 and Δn0 is large and positive.\n3. PS: the strongly polarizable phenyl group is a pendant group attached sideways, so polarizability is larger across the chain: p1 \u003c p2 and Δn0 = −0.16.\n4. PVC: the backbone C-C bonds (axial polarizability) and the C-Cl groups (mostly transverse) roughly compensate, so Δn0 ≈ 0.01.\n5. Sign for a drawn sample: Δn = Δn0 f with f \u003e 0 along the draw direction; for PET n_parallel \u003e n_perpendicular, for PS n_parallel \u003c n_perpendicular.","answers":["Δn0 ∝ (p1 − p2): positive when polarizable groups (phenylene) are in the backbone, negative when they hang sideways (PS), near zero when compensated (PVC)","For drawn PS the index along the draw direction is lower than across it; for PET and PC it is higher"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.2"},{"id":"9.3","title":"Crystalline orientation from an azimuthal X-ray scan","q":"The azimuthal intensity of an (hk0) wide-angle X-ray reflection of a uniaxially drawn semicrystalline polymer, with the director parallel to the average chain axis, is: φ (deg) = 0, 20, 60, 65, 70, 75, 80, 85, 90 and intensity I (arb. units) = 0, 0, 0, 2, 4, 8, 16, 40, 60. Evaluate \u003ccos²φ\u003e with a sum over the given points, I·cos²φ·sinφ divided by I·sinφ, and calculate the Hermans function of the (hk0) planes and the crystalline chain-axis orientation f_c. Assume orthorhombic crystals with a single interchain reflection family.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: azimuthal average with the sinφ weight, \u003ccos²φ\u003e = Σ I cosφ² sinφ / Σ I sinφ, over uniaxial orientation.\n2. Numerator terms (I cos²φ sinφ): 65°: 2(0.1786)(0.9063) = 0.324; 70°: 4(0.1170)(0.9397) = 0.440; 75°: 8(0.0670)(0.9659) = 0.518; 80°: 16(0.0302)(0.9848) = 0.476; 85°: 40(0.00760)(0.9962) = 0.303; 90°: 0. Sum = 2.060.\n3. Denominator (I sinφ): 1.813 + 3.759 + 7.727 + 15.757 + 39.848 + 60 = 128.90.\n4. \u003ccos²φ_hk0\u003e = 2.060/128.90 = 0.0160.\n5. f_hk0 = (3(0.0160) − 1)/2 = −0.476.\n6. Orthorhombic sum rule with one interchain reflection: 2 f_hk0 + f_001 = 0, so f_c = f_001 = −2 f_hk0 = 0.95.","answers":["\u003ccos²φ_hk0\u003e ≈ 0.016; f_hk0 ≈ −0.476","Crystalline chain-axis orientation f_c ≈ 0.95"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.3"},{"id":"9.4","title":"Amorphous orientation from overall and crystalline values","q":"For the polymer sample of the previous X-ray analysis the crystalline chain-axis orientation is f_c = 0.95. Optical (birefringence) measurements give an overall chain orientation f = 0.8 and DSC gives a mass crystallinity of 50 %. Calculate the amorphous chain-axis orientation f_a, and state which assumptions are made.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: with form and deformation birefringence neglected the birefringence is the weighted sum of the crystalline and amorphous contributions, Δn = Δn0 [w_c f_c + (1 − w_c) f_a], and f = Δn/Δn0.\n2. Therefore f = w_c f_c + (1 − w_c) f_a.\n3. Solve: f_a = (f − w_c f_c)/(1 − w_c).\n4. Values: f_a = (0.8 − 0.5 × 0.95)/0.5 = (0.8 − 0.475)/0.5 = 0.65.\n5. Assumptions: uniaxial orientation; the same intrinsic birefringence Δn0 for both phases; negligible form and stress birefringence; weights by mass fraction as given.","answers":["f_a ≈ 0.65 (lower than f_c = 0.95)"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.4"},{"id":"9.5","title":"Infrared dichroism of a drawn polyester fibre","q":"A uniaxially drawn polyester fibre is studied by polarized infrared spectroscopy at the carbonyl stretching band (1700 cm⁻¹), whose transition moment is perpendicular to the chain axis. (a) Sketch the absorbance as a function of the angle φ between the polarization direction of the light and the fibre axis. (b) Give the relation used to obtain the Hermans orientation function from the dichroic ratio. (c) With absorbances A∥ = 0.30 and A⊥ = 0.60 (polarization parallel and perpendicular to the fibre axis), find the chain-axis orientation.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: IR absorbance ∝ cos²κ, where κ is the angle between the electric vector and the transition moment M. M is perpendicular to the chain, so absorption is a minimum when E is along the fibre axis and a maximum when E is perpendicular.\n2. (a) A(φ) has its minimum at φ = 0° (polarization parallel to the fibre) and its maximum at φ = 90°, a smooth curve ∝ sin²φ for perfect orientation (cos² shape shifted by 90°); weaker modulation for lower orientation.\n3. (b) Dichroic ratio R = A∥/A⊥. General relation f = (R − 1)(R0 + 2)/((R + 2)(R0 − 1)), with R0 = 2 cot²ψ for perfect orientation; for a perpendicular moment ψ = 90°, R0 = 0, giving f = 2(1 − R)/(R + 2).\n4. (c) R = 0.30/0.60 = 0.50. f = 2(1 − 0.5)/(0.5 + 2) = 1.0/2.5 = 0.40.\n5. Interpretation: f = 0.4 means moderate chain-axis orientation (f = 0 is random, 1 perfect).","answers":["(a) minimum at φ = 0°, maximum at φ = 90° (moment perpendicular to the chain)","(b) f = 2(1 − R)/(R + 2), R = A∥/A⊥","(c) f = 0.40"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.7"},{"id":"9.6","title":"Extrusion of two semicrystalline polymers below Tm","q":"Why is it impossible to extrude polyamide 6 through a very narrow die at temperatures well below its melting point, while the same operation is possible for polyethylene? Name the structural feature responsible, the other requirements for obtaining an ultra-oriented fibre by solid-state extrusion or cold drawing, and the typical temperature window.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: high draw ratios in the solid state require that the crystals themselves deform plastically (chain slip), not only the amorphous layers.\n2. Polyethylene has a crystalline α relaxation (chain slip through the crystal along (hk0) planes parallel to the chain axis), so crystals deform plastically and lamellae transform into fibrils; draw ratio up to about 40.\n3. Polyamide 6 has no crystalline α process and its crystals are held by strong hydrogen bonds between chains, so crystals do not yield by chain slip and it can only be drawn or extruded to small draw ratios.\n4. Other requirements: few chain entanglements (crystallisation conditions giving few entanglements favour extensibility), enough interlamellar tie chains to avoid early brittle fracture, and strain hardening that stabilises the neck.\n5. Window: between the onset temperature of the α process and the melting point (PE: extrusion 80-110 °C; drawing 10-40 °C below Tm).","answers":["PE has a crystalline α relaxation (crystal chain slip); PA6 does not and is hydrogen bonded","Also few entanglements, enough tie chains, strain hardening; temperature between α onset and Tm"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 9.9"},{"id":"9.7","title":"Intrinsic birefringence of crystals and amorphous phase from data","q":"The birefringence of a semicrystalline oriented polymer follows Δn = x f_c Δn_c⁰ + (1 − x) f_a Δn_a⁰, where x is the volume fraction of crystals, f_c and f_a are the orientation factors of the crystal and amorphous phases, and Δn_c⁰, Δn_a⁰ the birefringences of the fully oriented phases. Data for uniaxially drawn polypropylene (draw ratio: x, f_a, f_c, 1000 Δn): 1.2: 0.65, 0.05, 0.06, 2.2; 1.5: 0.60, 0.06, 0.15, 4.0; 4: 0.60, 0.27, 0.71, 19; 5: 0.62, 0.28, 0.87, 22; 6: 0.60, 0.46, 0.92, 27. Estimate Δn_c⁰ and Δn_a⁰.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the equation is linear in the two unknowns, Δn = A·(x f_c) + B·((1 − x) f_a) with A = Δn_c⁰, B = Δn_a⁰; fit by least squares to the 5 data rows.\n2. Build the regressors (x f_c, (1 − x) f_a, Δn): (0.039, 0.0175, 0.0022); (0.090, 0.024, 0.0040); (0.426, 0.108, 0.019); (0.539, 0.106, 0.022); (0.552, 0.184, 0.027).\n3. Normal equations (least squares) give A ≈ 0.029, B ≈ 0.059; a graphical fit gives the book values 0.028 and 0.063.\n4. Check with the last row: 0.552(0.028) + 0.184(0.063) = 0.0155 + 0.0116 = 0.027, matching the measured 0.027.","answers":["Δn_c⁰ ≈ 0.03 (book: 0.028)","Δn_a⁰ ≈ 0.06 (book: 0.063)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.21"},{"id":"9.8","title":"Orientation factor of an affinely stretched rubber","q":"An isotropic rubber is stretched uniaxially at constant volume to a draw ratio λ = 3.0. Chain units initially at angle φ1 to the draw direction rotate affinely, like lines joining pairs of points in the rubber, to an angle φ2 with tan φ2 = λ^(−3/2) tan φ1. Assuming the initial unit orientations are random, calculate the orientation factor f = (3\u003ccos²φ2\u003e − 1)/2.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Derivation of the rotation: a unit with components (cosφ1, sinφ1) deforms to (λ cosφ1, λ^(−1/2) sinφ1) at constant volume, so tanφ2 = λ^(−3/2) tanφ1.\n2. Express cos²φ2 = λ³u²/(1 + (λ³ − 1)u²) with u = cosφ1.\n3. Random initial distribution: u uniform on [0, 1], so \u003ccos²φ2\u003e = ∫0^1 λ³u²/(1 + (λ³ − 1)u²) du = (λ³/(λ³ − 1)) [1 − arctan(√(λ³ − 1))/√(λ³ − 1)].\n4. λ = 3: λ³ = 27, √26 = 5.099, arctan(5.099) = 1.377 rad. \u003ccos²φ2\u003e = (27/26)(1 − 1.377/5.099) = 1.0385 × 0.7299 = 0.758.\n5. f = (3 × 0.758 − 1)/2 = 0.637.","answers":["f ≈ 0.64 (\u003ccos²φ2\u003e ≈ 0.758)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 2.23"},{"id":"10.1","title":"Counting lattice arrangements: molecules versus chains","q":"(a) In how many ways can 10 identical solute molecules, each occupying one site, be arranged in a lattice of 100 sites? (b) In how many ways can one oligomer of 10 repeat units (each unit one site, units on adjacent sites) be placed in a lattice of 100 sites, taking the coordination number as z = 6 and using the number of ways 100·z·(z − 1)^8? (c) Calculate the corresponding combinatorial entropies k ln P in units of k and state the main difference between mixtures of two low-molar-mass species and a polymer-small-molecule mixture.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Boltzmann entropy S = k ln P with P the number of arrangements.\n2. (a) Identical point-like molecules: P1 = 100!/(10! 90!) = 1.73 × 10¹³.\n3. (b) First segment: 100 sites; second: z = 6 neighbours; each following segment: z − 1 = 5 (one neighbour taken): P2 = 100 × 6 × 5⁸ = 2.34 × 10⁸.\n4. (c) k ln P1 = k ln(1.73 × 10¹³) = 30.5 k; k ln P2 = k ln(2.34 × 10⁸) = 19.3 k.\n5. Difference: linking the segments into a chain removes independent placements, so the combinatorial entropy of mixing per lattice site is much lower for chains than for the same number of separate molecules. For a polymer/solvent mixture, ΔS per site ∝ 1/x, whereas for two low-molar-mass liquids (symmetric case) it is −R(x₁ln x₁ + x₂ ln x₂); the polymer-solvent system also has an asymmetric phase diagram with higher critical temperature.","answers":["(a) 1.73 × 10¹³ arrangements","(b) about 2.34 × 10⁸ arrangements","(c) ΔS = 30.5 k versus 19.3 k; chain connectivity strongly reduces the entropy of mixing (∝ 1/N)"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.1"},{"id":"10.2","title":"Binodal and spinodal of a regular solution","q":"A binary regular solution has ΔG_mix/N = B x₁x₂ + RT(x₁ ln x₁ + x₂ ln x₂) with B = 8 kJ/mol (R = 8.314 J/mol K). Find the upper critical solution temperature, and at T = 300 K and T = 375 K the spinodal and binodal compositions x₁ (lower values; the other branch is 1 − x₁). Describe the T-x₁ phase diagram.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the binodal is the common-tangent condition, which for a symmetric ΔG is the condition of zero first derivative; the spinodal is d²(ΔG/N)/dx₁² = 0.\n2. Spinodal: d²/dx₁² = −2B + RT[1/x₁ + 1/(1 − x₁)] = 0 gives x₁(1 − x₁) = RT/2B, x₁,spin = ½ ± [¼ − RT/2B]^½.\n3. Critical point: x₁ = ½ and RT_c/2B = ¼ gives T_c = B/2R = 8000/(2 × 8.314) = 481 K.\n4. T = 300 K: RT/2B = 0.1559, so x₁,spin = ½ − √(0.25 − 0.1559) = 0.193 (and 0.807).\n5. T = 375 K: RT/2B = 0.1949, x₁,spin = ½ − √0.0551 = 0.265 (and 0.735).\n6. Binodal (symmetric): d(ΔG/N)/dx₁ = B(1 − 2x₁) + RT ln[x₁/(1 − x₁)] = 0, solved numerically: x₁,bin = 0.054 at 300 K and 0.131 at 375 K (and 1 − x₁).\n7. Diagram: dome-shaped UCST diagram, symmetric about x₁ = 0.5, with binodal outside the spinodal; the two curves meet at the critical point (481 K, 0.5); above T_c the system is single-phase for all compositions.","answers":["T_c = B/2R ≈ 481 K at x₁ = 0.5","300 K: spinodal 0.193/0.807, binodal 0.054/0.946","375 K: spinodal 0.265/0.735, binodal 0.131/0.869","UCST dome, binodal outside spinodal, meeting at the critical point"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.2"},{"id":"10.3","title":"Spinodal composition from regular solution theory","q":"Derive an expression for the spinodal composition x₁ of the regular solution ΔG_mix/N = B x₁(1 − x₁) + RT[x₁ ln x₁ + (1 − x₁) ln(1 − x₁)] as a function of T and B, show that the spinodal points are symmetric about x₁ = ½, and give the condition under which the metastable and unstable regions vanish.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the spinodal is the locus of zero curvature of the free energy of mixing.\n2. First derivative: d(ΔG/N)/dx₁ = B − 2Bx₁ + RT[ln x₁ − ln(1 − x₁)].\n3. Second derivative: d²(ΔG/N)/dx₁² = −2B + RT[1/x₁ + 1/(1 − x₁)] = −2B + RT/[x₁(1 − x₁)].\n4. Set to zero: x₁(1 − x₁) = RT/2B, i.e. x₁² − x₁ + RT/2B = 0.\n5. Solution: x₁,spin = ½ ± [¼ − RT/2B]^½. The two roots add to 1, so they are symmetric about x₁ = ½.\n6. Real roots require RT/2B ≤ ¼, i.e. T ≤ T_c = B/2R. At T_c the roots merge at x₁ = ½ (critical point, also d³ΔG/dx₁³ = 0 there); above T_c the curvature is positive for all x₁ and the solution is stable at every composition.","answers":["x₁,spin = ½ ± √(¼ − RT/2B)","symmetric about ½; spinodal exists only for T \u003c T_c = B/2R"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.3"},{"id":"10.4","title":"Critical parameters of a polymer solution versus chain length","q":"For a mixture of a small-molecule solvent (1) and a polymer (2) of x segments, with Flory-Huggins free energy ΔG_mix/RT per site = v₁ ln v₁ + (v₂/x) ln v₂ + χ v₁v₂, the critical point is at v₂,c = 1/(1 + √x) and χ_c = ½ + 1/√x + 1/(2x). With χ = B/RT and B = 2 kJ/mol, calculate v₂,c, χ_c and the critical temperature T_c = B/(Rχ_c) for x = 10, 100, 1000 and x → ∞, and describe how the phase diagram shifts with molar mass.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: insert x into the given critical expressions; T_c from χ_c = B/RT_c.\n2. x = 10: v₂,c = 1/(1 + 3.162) = 0.240; χ_c = 0.5 + 0.316 + 0.05 = 0.866; T_c = 2000/(8.314 × 0.866) = 278 K.\n3. x = 100: v₂,c = 1/11 = 0.091; χ_c = 0.5 + 0.1 + 0.005 = 0.605; T_c = 398 K.\n4. x = 1000: v₂,c = 0.031; χ_c = 0.5 + 0.0316 + 0.0005 = 0.532; T_c = 452 K.\n5. x → ∞: v₂,c → 0, χ_c → ½, T_c = 2B/R = 481 K (the θ temperature).\n6. Trends: with increasing molar mass the critical composition moves to very low polymer fraction (asymmetric diagram), the critical χ falls toward ½, and the UCST rises and saturates at θ.","answers":["x = 10: 0.240, 0.866, 278 K","x = 100: 0.091, 0.605, 398 K","x = 1000: 0.031, 0.532, 452 K","x → ∞: v₂,c → 0, χ_c → 0.5, T_c → 481 K","Higher molar mass: critical point moves to low v₂ and higher T, diagram becomes asymmetric"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.4"},{"id":"10.5","title":"Solubility of PVC in its own monomer","q":"The Hansen solubility parameters (MPa^½) of poly(vinyl chloride) are δ_D = 18.2, δ_P = 7.5, δ_H = 8.3 with an interaction-sphere radius R_A0 = 3.5; those of vinyl chloride (VC) are δ_D = 15.4, δ_P = 8.1, δ_H = 2.4. Using R_A² = 4(Δδ_D)² + (Δδ_P)² + (Δδ_H)², show whether PVC is miscible with VC.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: the Hansen distance between polymer and liquid in (2δ_D, δ_P, δ_H) space is compared with the polymer\u0027s interaction radius; R_A/R_A0 \u003c 1 means solvent.\n2. Differences: Δδ_D = 18.2 − 15.4 = 2.8, Δδ_P = 7.5 − 8.1 = −0.6, Δδ_H = 8.3 − 2.4 = 5.9.\n3. R_A² = 4(2.8)² + (0.6)² + (5.9)² = 31.36 + 0.36 + 34.81 = 66.53, R_A = 8.2 MPa^½.\n4. Ratio R_A/R_A0 = 8.2/3.5 = 2.3 \u003e 1: VC lies outside the solubility sphere, so it is a non-solvent.\n5. Conclusion: PVC does not dissolve in (is not miscible with) its monomer, giving the characteristic particle structure of PVC.","answers":["R_A ≈ 8.2 MPa^½, R_A/R_A0 ≈ 2.3 \u003e 1","PVC is immiscible with VC"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.7"},{"id":"10.6","title":"Miscible blend with elevated glass transition","q":"A miscible polymer blend shows a glass transition temperature higher than that of either pure polymer. Suggest a molecular explanation and an experiment that would critically test it. Explain also why such strongly interacting pairs are the typical route to miscibility in terms of the Flory-Huggins free energy.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: relate T_g to mobility; a blend T_g above both components means that mixing reduces segmental mobility.\n2. Hypothesis: strong specific interaction between the two polymers (hydrogen bonding or charge-transfer / donor-acceptor complex between groups of different polarity) acts like physical crosslinking.\n3. Test: infrared spectroscopy should show frequency shifts of the groups involved (e.g. −OH near 3500-3600 cm⁻¹, C=O near 1700-1750 cm⁻¹); NMR relaxation or a temperature series that shows the T_g enhancement vanishing when the interaction is broken would also test it.\n4. Flory-Huggins link: with N ~ 10³ the entropy term is negligible, so ΔG_mix \u003c 0 needs χ ≲ χ_c = 2/N or negative; specific interactions make u_AB more attractive than the mean of u_AA and u_BB, giving a negative χ (ΔH_mix \u003c 0).","answers":["Strong specific interactions (H-bonds, charge transfer) between the components restrict mobility","Test with IR band shifts (or NMR)","Specific interactions give χ \u003c 0, which is the only way a high-N blend can mix"],"src":"Gedde Exercise 4.8"},{"id":"10.7","title":"Free energy of mixing is negative yet the blend is unstable","q":"Two polymers with N_A = N_B = 800 segments are blended 50/50 by volume. Their interaction parameter is χ = 1.0/T with T in K. (a) Find χ_c and the UCST of the blend. (b) At T = 350 K evaluate ΔG_mix/(nkT) at φ_A = 0.5 and the curvature ∂²(ΔG_mix/nkT)/∂φ² there, and decide whether the blend is stable. (c) What UCST results for N_A = N_B = 200?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: use ΔG/nkT = (φ/N_A) ln φ + ((1 − φ)/N_B) ln(1 − φ) + χφ(1 − φ) and χ_c = ½(N_A^−½ + N_B^−½)² = 2/N for equal N.\n2. (a) χ_c = 2/800 = 0.0025. T_c = 1.0/0.0025 = 400 K (UCST, symmetric blend at φ_c = 0.5).\n3. (b) At 350 K, χ = 1.0/350 = 0.002857. Entropic part: 2 × (0.5/800) ln 0.5 = −8.66 × 10⁻⁴; enthalpic part: χ/4 = 7.14 × 10⁻⁴. ΔG/nkT = −1.5 × 10⁻⁴ \u003c 0.\n4. Curvature: 1/(Nφ) + 1/(N(1 − φ)) − 2χ = 0.0025 + 0.0025 − 0.005714 = −7.1 × 10⁻⁴ \u003c 0.\n5. Result: ΔG_mix is negative at φ = 0.5, but the curvature is negative, so the blend is unstable (inside the spinodal, T \u003c T_c) and decomposes spontaneously; ΔG_mix \u003c 0 is necessary, not sufficient.\n6. (c) N = 200: χ_c = 0.01, T_c = 1.0/0.01 = 100 K, i.e. miscible at all practical temperatures: the UCST falls strongly as N decreases.","answers":["(a) χ_c = 0.0025, UCST = 400 K","(b) ΔG/nkT ≈ −1.5 × 10⁻⁴ but curvature ≈ −7 × 10⁻⁴ \u003c 0: unstable (spinodal decomposition)","(c) UCST = 100 K for N = 200"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"10.8","title":"Why some blends demix on heating","q":"Flory-Huggins theory with a purely enthalpic, temperature-independent-energy χ ∝ 1/T predicts only an upper critical solution temperature, but many polymer blends phase-separate on heating. Explain two physical mechanisms for such lower critical solution temperatures, sketch χ(T) against the critical value for a hydrogen-bonding blend, and explain how a closed miscibility loop can form.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: combinatorial entropy always favours mixing, so heating-induced demixing must come from the enthalpy (free-volume) side.\n2. Mechanism 1, compressibility/free volume: components differ in thermal expansion; equation-of-state theories (Flory-Orwoll-Vrij, Sanchez-Lacombe lattice fluid, hole model with vacancies) show that free-volume mismatch at higher T raises the effective χ, so demixing occurs on heating.\n3. Mechanism 2, directional interactions: hydrogen bonds are strongly angle dependent. Write χ(T) = χ_disp + λ(T) χ_spec with χ_spec \u003c 0 and weighting λ(T) falling as T rises.\n4. Sketch: χ(T) starts below χ_c (specific part dominates, miscible), rises with T and crosses χ_c at the LCST; at still higher T χ → χ_disp \u003e 0 (dispersion only, immiscible).\n5. UCST branch at the dispersion-controlled side usually lies above the degradation temperature, so it is not observed; lowering the UCST (shorter chains) and raising the LCST (more stable bonding) until they meet gives a closed miscibility loop.","answers":["Compressibility (free-volume mismatch) and temperature-dependent directional interactions (H-bond weight λ(T))","χ(T) crosses χ_c from below on heating giving an LCST","Closed loop: UCST lowered by smaller N while LCST raised until they intersect"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"11.1","title":"Critical point of an asymmetric polymer blend","q":"A binary blend has a Flory-Huggins free energy of mixing per lattice site ΔG/nkT = (φ/N_A) ln φ + ((1 − φ)/N_B) ln(1 − φ) + χφ(1 − φ), where φ is the volume fraction of A. (a) Derive the critical composition φ_c and critical interaction parameter χ_c from the conditions that the second and third derivatives vanish. (b) Evaluate them for N_A = 500 and N_B = 2000. (c) If χ = 12/T (T in K), what is the critical temperature (UCST)?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: critical point = point where spinodal (∂²f/∂φ² = 0) and ∂³f/∂φ³ = 0 coincide.\n2. Second derivative: f\u0027\u0027 = 1/(N_Aφ) + 1/(N_B(1 − φ)) − 2χ = 0.\n3. Third derivative: f\u0027\u0027\u0027 = −1/(N_Aφ²) + 1/(N_B(1 − φ)²) = 0, so ((1 − φ)/φ)² = N_A/N_B and φ_c = √N_B/(√N_A + √N_B).\n4. Insert into f\u0027\u0027 = 0: χ_c = ½(1/√N_A + 1/√N_B)².\n5. (b) √500 = 22.36, √2000 = 44.72: φ_c = 44.72/67.08 = 0.667; χ_c = ½(0.04472 + 0.02236)² = ½(0.06708)² = 0.00225.\n6. (c) T_c = 12/0.00225 = 5.3 × 10³ K, far above any degradation temperature: the blend would be immiscible at all accessible temperatures (the UCST is not observable).","answers":["φ_c = √N_B/(√N_A + √N_B), χ_c = ½(N_A^−½ + N_B^−½)²","φ_c(A) = 0.667 (critical composition is rich in the shorter chain A), χ_c = 0.00225","T_c ≈ 5300 K: above the ceiling (degradation) temperature, immiscible in practice"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"11.2","title":"Spinodal compositions of a symmetric blend","q":"A symmetric blend of two polymers with N_A = N_B = 100 has χ = 0.03 at the temperature of interest. Using the spinodal condition of Flory-Huggins theory, (a) compare χ with the critical value, and (b) find the spinodal compositions. Is a 50/50 blend stable at this temperature?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: spinodal: 1/(Nφ) + 1/(N(1 − φ)) = 2χ for equal N.\n2. (a) χ_c = 2/N = 0.02 \u003c χ = 0.03: the temperature is below the UCST, so a two-phase region exists.\n3. (b) 1/(Nφ(1 − φ)) = 2χ gives φ(1 − φ) = 1/(2Nχ) = 1/6 = 0.1667.\n4. Solve φ² − φ + 0.1667 = 0: φ = ½ ± √(0.25 − 0.1667) = 0.5 ± 0.289 = 0.211 and 0.789.\n5. 50/50 lies between 0.211 and 0.789, i.e. inside the spinodal (curvature = 1/(Nφ) + 1/(N(1 − φ)) − 2χ = 0.04 − 0.06 = −0.02 \u003c 0), so the blend is unstable and decomposes spinodally.","answers":["(a) χ_c = 0.02 \u003c 0.03: phase-separating","(b) spinodal at φ = 0.211 and 0.789","50/50 blend is unstable (curvature −0.02)"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"11.3","title":"Separation mechanism and phase fractions after a quench","q":"For the blend of the previous problem (N_A = N_B = 100, χ = 0.03) the binodal compositions are φ = 0.071 and 0.929 and the spinodal compositions are 0.211 and 0.789. A homogeneous blend is quenched to this temperature. (a) For overall composition φ = 0.15 and for φ = 0.50 name the separation mechanism and the expected initial morphology. (b) For φ = 0.15 find the equilibrium phase fractions by the lever rule. (c) Why does the system not reach two layers in practice?","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: compare the overall composition with binodal and spinodal points; curvature negative inside the spinodal (unstable), positive but free energy above the common tangent between binodal and spinodal (metastable).\n2. (a) φ = 0.15: lies between binodal (0.071) and spinodal (0.211): metastable, phase separation needs nucleation and growth (activated process, nuclei of composition near 0.93 growing as droplets of the minority phase in an A-poor matrix of composition ≈ 0.071). φ = 0.50: inside the spinodal: spontaneous spinodal decomposition, no barrier, fine bicontinuous (co-continuous) structure of nearly uniform periodicity.\n3. (b) Lever rule at φ = 0.15 with coexisting 0.071 and 0.929: fraction of the A-rich phase = (0.15 − 0.071)/(0.929 − 0.071) = 0.079/0.858 = 0.092; fraction of the A-poor phase = 0.908.\n4. (c) Both mechanisms are limited by chain diffusion and viscosity; the structure coarsens slowly and is arrested by vitrification or crystallisation, so a finite-size morphology is retained.","answers":["φ = 0.15: nucleation and growth (droplets); φ = 0.50: spinodal decomposition (bicontinuous)","Phase fractions: 9.2 % A-rich phase (φ = 0.929) and 90.8 % A-poor phase (φ = 0.071)","Slow diffusion/high viscosity and freezing of the structure prevent complete separation"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"11.4","title":"Glass transition of a miscible blend by the Fox equation","q":"A miscible blend is made from polystyrene (T_g = 373 K) and poly(2,6-dimethyl-1,4-phenylene oxide) (T_g = 480 K). Using the Fox equation, (a) find the T_g of a 50/50 blend by mass, (b) find the mass fraction of PS required for T_g = 400 K, and (c) state what two glass transitions in a DSC trace of such a blend would indicate and what restriction applies to the use of T_g for assessing miscibility.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: Fox equation 1/T_g = w₁/T_g1 + w₂/T_g2 (T in K, w mass fractions).\n2. (a) 1/T_g = 0.5/373 + 0.5/480 = 0.0013405 + 0.0010417 = 0.0023822 K⁻¹, T_g = 419.8 K ≈ 420 K.\n3. (b) 1/400 = 0.0025 = w/373 + (1 − w)/480 = 0.0020833 + w(0.0026810 − 0.0020833); 0.0004167 = 0.0005977 w; w = 0.697.\n4. (c) Two T_g values at the pure-component positions mean the blend is phase-separated on a scale above the technique\u0027s resolution (about 2-15 nm); partial miscibility gives two shifted T_g values. The method requires ΔT_g \u003e about 20 K and a minor component above about 20 % (broad transitions limit it).","answers":["(a) T_g ≈ 420 K","(b) w_PS ≈ 0.70","(c) two T_g = phase separation at scales \u003e 2-15 nm; method needs ΔT_g \u003e 20 K"],"src":"course-style"},{"id":"11.5","title":"Thermoplastic elastomer: why it is elastic yet processable","q":"A triblock copolymer polystyrene-polybutadiene-polystyrene (SBS) with 25 wt% styrene is elastic at room temperature, yet can be injection moulded at 170 °C and re-used. Explain the structure responsible, give the service temperature window (T_g of PB about −90 °C, T_g of PS about 95 °C), say what would happen at 50 wt% styrene, and why such a thermoplastic rubber can toughen polypropylene.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: immiscible blocks of a block copolymer segregate into microphases, with the glassy PS acting as physical crosslinks.\n2. Structure: the glassy PS end blocks of different molecules aggregate into small domains; the long PB mid-blocks form a rubbery matrix, anchored at both ends in different PS domains, a physically crosslinked network (linear or branched molecules).\n3. Window: between T_g(PB) ≈ −90 °C (below which the rubber is glassy) and T_g(PS) ≈ 95 °C (above which the domains soften). Above the PS T_g (e.g. 170 °C) the domains liquefy and the material flows; on cooling the domains reform: reprocessable, unlike thermoset rubber.\n4. At 50 wt% styrene the morphology changes to co-continuous or lamellar glassy domains: the material becomes a stiff, leathery thermoplastic, not a rubber (the glassy fraction carries load).\n5. PP toughening: the TPE is mixed into PP in ordinary thermoplastic equipment and gives finely dispersed rubbery phase (alloy) that toughens the matrix like other rubber modifiers.","answers":["PS end-block domains act as physical crosslinks in a PB rubbery matrix","Usable between about −90 °C and 95 °C; flows above 95 °C and reforms on cooling","At 50 % styrene: stiff thermoplastic with co-continuous/lamellar glassy domains","Dispersed rubbery TPE phase toughens PP"],"src":"McCrum §3.6 (course-style)"},{"id":"11.6","title":"Minimum toughening temperature in a Charpy test","q":"An ethylene-propylene rubber (T_g = −50 °C) toughens nylon 6,6: in slow bending tests (time to peak load 1 s) the blend first shows a marked rise in fracture resistance at −50 °C, when the rubber particles have time to stress-relax. Using the WLF shift factor log a_T = −17.4 (T − T_g)/(51.6 + T − T_g), calculate the minimum temperature at which toughening occurs in the Charpy impact test, where the time to peak load is 1 ms.","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: toughening needs the rubber relaxation time to be shorter than the test time. The reference (1 s, −50 °C = T_g) fixes the required relaxation time; a 1000 times faster test needs a 1000 times faster relaxation, i.e. a_T = 10⁻³ relative to T_g.\n2. Set log a_T = log(10⁻³) = −3.\n3. −3 = −17.4 ΔT/(51.6 + ΔT) with ΔT = T − T_g: 3(51.6 + ΔT) = 17.4 ΔT.\n4. 154.8 = 14.4 ΔT, ΔT = 10.75 °C.\n5. T = −50 + 10.75 = −39.3 °C.","answers":["T ≈ −39 °C (−39.3 °C)"],"src":"McCrum Problem 5.27"},{"id":"11.7","title":"Design of a rubber-toughened glassy polymer","q":"You must toughen a brittle glassy polymer (polystyrene) with a rubber. List the roles of (a) rubber content, (b) particle size and internal structure, (c) crosslinking and grafting, and (d) rubber glass transition temperature, and state the penalty paid and the two principal toughening mechanisms (crazing-resistant versus craze-prone matrices).","figs":[],"figDesc":"(no figure)","sol":"1. Approach: rubber particles lower the yield stress, cavitate under the triaxial crack-tip stress and let the matrix deform plastically, which converts brittle fracture into energy-absorbing yielding or crazing.\n2. (a) 5-20 % rubber gives worthwhile toughening; more rubber lowers modulus and yield strength (the penalty: stiffness and strength drop).\n3. (b) Particles 50 nm to 10 μm; for craze-prone PS (and SAN) particles of 1-5 μm with \u0027salami\u0027 morphology (PS sub-inclusions in rubber) cavitate at low stress and fibrillate, bridging craze fibrils (strain hardening) so that multiple crazing absorbs energy; small well-bonded particles favour shear yielding in craze-resistant polymers (PC, nylon, epoxy) through cavitation and dilatational shear bands.\n4. (c) Rubber is crosslinked to survive melt processing and grafted to the matrix for interface strength; poorly bonded or very large voids nucleate cracks and embrittle.\n5. (d) The rubber T_g (about −80 °C for PB) must lie well below the use temperature, and the rubber must relax to a modulus of about 1 MPa within the loading time (impact: shifts the brittle-tough transition up, e.g. −75 °C slow loading to −10 °C for a fast crack in ABS).","answers":["Rubber content 5-20 %, price: lower modulus and yield stress","Particle size 1-5 μm with salami structure for PS (multiple crazing); small particles for shear yielding in craze-resistant matrices","Crosslinked rubber grafted to matrix; rubber T_g well below service temperature and rubber relaxation faster than the loading time"],"src":"McCrum §5.7 (course-style)"}];
